WEBVTT

1
00:00:06,798 --> 00:00:08,675
[solemn music playing]

2
00:00:08,758 --> 00:00:13,179
[man] Going against a big company
like Boeing does not happen every day.

3
00:00:13,263 --> 00:00:14,222
[beeps]

4
00:00:14,305 --> 00:00:15,557
[woman] Raise your right hand.

5
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,309
Do you solemnly swear the testimony
you're about to give is the truth,

6
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:20,103
the whole truth and nothing but the truth?

7
00:00:20,186 --> 00:00:21,021
I do.

8
00:00:22,731 --> 00:00:25,316
{\an8}Boeing will fight you to the bitter end.

9
00:00:25,817 --> 00:00:31,489
So what he was instructing people to do
was violate rules and regulations

10
00:00:31,573 --> 00:00:36,327
that they could be actually held liable,
criminally charged for.

11
00:00:37,704 --> 00:00:40,874
We could very well be putting
the public at risk.

12
00:00:40,957 --> 00:00:44,836
{\an8}The intensity of the implications
of what he was testifying about

13
00:00:44,919 --> 00:00:46,838
made me take a pause.

14
00:00:47,964 --> 00:00:50,091
John was a brave guy for speaking out.

15
00:00:51,009 --> 00:00:55,847
But I personally thought to myself
that this shit could get somebody killed.

16
00:00:56,347 --> 00:00:57,515
[thunder rumbles]

17
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:04,230
We were scheduled for John to continue
the deposition the next morning.

18
00:01:04,731 --> 00:01:06,608
[solemn music continues]

19
00:01:06,691 --> 00:01:10,612
But at ten o'clock,
John had not arrived yet.

20
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:14,157
So I called the hotel…

21
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:15,075
[phone ringing]

22
00:01:15,158 --> 00:01:17,577
…and asked to be put through to his room.

23
00:01:18,661 --> 00:01:20,497
And the phone just rang.

24
00:01:22,082 --> 00:01:23,917
I asked if someone can go up

25
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,128
and do a check
to see if he was still sleeping.

26
00:01:27,712 --> 00:01:29,839
They said, "No, he's not there."

27
00:01:31,174 --> 00:01:33,676
I asked, "Is his truck outside?"

28
00:01:34,177 --> 00:01:40,308
And the manager came back on the phone
and said she had gone out to his truck

29
00:01:40,391 --> 00:01:42,936
and looked at him from the passenger side.

30
00:01:44,771 --> 00:01:47,607
And that's when she called 911.

31
00:01:51,903 --> 00:01:52,946
[solemn music fades]

32
00:01:53,029 --> 00:01:55,198
-[siren wailing]
-[dramatic music playing]

33
00:01:55,281 --> 00:01:58,660
{\an8}[reporter 1] <i>Boeing has given the world</i>
<i>yet another reason to fear flying.</i>

34
00:01:58,743 --> 00:02:01,788
{\an8}<i>Several of the company's</i>
<i>new Dreamliner planes have caught on fire.</i>

35
00:02:01,871 --> 00:02:05,792
{\an8}[reporter 2] <i>One of the Boeing 777's</i>
<i>engines failed minutes after takeoff.</i>

36
00:02:05,875 --> 00:02:11,047
{\an8}[man] <i>And this is my parents' house</i>
<i>after a 777 engine blew up in the sky.</i>

37
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:13,842
{\an8}[reporter 3] <i>A hundred</i>
<i>and twenty-nine passengers were evacuated.</i>

38
00:02:13,925 --> 00:02:15,552
{\an8}<i>Five people were injured.</i>

39
00:02:15,635 --> 00:02:19,055
{\an8}[reporter 4] <i>Fifty injured</i>
<i>after a flight lost control midair.</i>

40
00:02:19,139 --> 00:02:22,016
{\an8}[reporter 5] <i>One passenger</i>
<i>was killed from an explosion.</i>

41
00:02:22,517 --> 00:02:26,229
{\an8}[reporter 6] <i>These are incredibly</i>
<i>dangerous things that continue to happen.</i>

42
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,733
{\an8}[reporter 7] <i>When you see news story</i>
<i>after news story, incident after incident,</i>

43
00:02:30,817 --> 00:02:33,069
{\an8}<i>you wonder how Boeing is still in the air.</i>

44
00:02:33,153 --> 00:02:34,904
{\an8}[dramatic music continues]

45
00:02:43,246 --> 00:02:45,665
[music fades]

46
00:02:51,796 --> 00:02:54,799
{\an8}I'm that kid that still looks up
at airplanes every time I hear one.

47
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:57,010
{\an8}"Oh my God!" [chuckles] Yeah.

48
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:04,434
I always loved aviation.

49
00:03:04,517 --> 00:03:06,144
[intriguing music playing]

50
00:03:06,227 --> 00:03:09,230
My dad worked on a F-14.

51
00:03:10,106 --> 00:03:13,401
So I always would go to the gates
and watch the airplanes fly off.

52
00:03:15,528 --> 00:03:17,655
Growing up in the Seattle area,
I always thought

53
00:03:17,739 --> 00:03:19,699
that I would eventually work at Boeing.

54
00:03:21,534 --> 00:03:24,329
I was very happy when Boeing recruited me
out of school.

55
00:03:25,747 --> 00:03:27,749
{\an8}Boeing was a fantastic company.

56
00:03:28,917 --> 00:03:32,795
If you had your Boeing shirt on, people
would come up to you and talk to you.

57
00:03:33,421 --> 00:03:34,756
It was just a sense of pride.

58
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:35,673
[engine rumbles]

59
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:38,718
[launch announcer] <i>We have ignition.</i>
<i>We have liftoff.</i>

60
00:03:39,594 --> 00:03:42,055
[man 1] I was a rocket scientist
for a long time,

61
00:03:42,138 --> 00:03:45,433
but I wanted to move to airplanes.

62
00:03:46,392 --> 00:03:48,228
{\an8}And I wanted to work

63
00:03:48,311 --> 00:03:50,730
{\an8}in the best airplane manufacturer
in the world.

64
00:03:51,773 --> 00:03:55,360
{\an8}[reporter 1] <i>Over a century, Boeing</i>
<i>transformed travel across the globe.</i>

65
00:03:55,443 --> 00:03:58,363
{\an8}[reporter 2] <i>The biggest</i>
<i>aerospace company in the world.</i>

66
00:03:58,446 --> 00:04:00,573
{\an8}Really, I was proud working for Boeing.

67
00:04:00,657 --> 00:04:02,742
I'm still proud working for Boeing.

68
00:04:04,118 --> 00:04:05,954
[man 2] Boeing changed my life.

69
00:04:06,704 --> 00:04:08,748
{\an8}We were really poor growing up.

70
00:04:09,874 --> 00:04:13,544
And the financial trajectory
just totally changed.

71
00:04:13,628 --> 00:04:19,092
Working at Boeing, to have a job there,
it was really a badge of honor.

72
00:04:19,175 --> 00:04:20,218
[dramatic music playing]

73
00:04:20,301 --> 00:04:23,596
[narrator] <i>This is Boeing.</i>
<i>They build airplanes.</i>

74
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:26,182
<i>And always there is one idea,</i>

75
00:04:26,266 --> 00:04:31,104
<i>make everything just a little better</i>
<i>than it really needs to be.</i>

76
00:04:32,897 --> 00:04:38,194
[Meyers] Boeing Company was very unique
in that if there was ever a dispute,

77
00:04:38,278 --> 00:04:40,905
the quality department
made the final decision

78
00:04:40,989 --> 00:04:43,741
as to whether or not
to sign off on the work.

79
00:04:43,825 --> 00:04:46,160
-[man] Okay, so we're ready to go, right?
-Yes.

80
00:04:46,661 --> 00:04:50,665
You had, like, two or three eyes
on everything that you did

81
00:04:50,748 --> 00:04:53,710
to just make sure it was accurate.

82
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:56,170
I don't think that's gonna work.
I really don't.

83
00:04:56,254 --> 00:04:59,132
[Mohawk] Back in those days,
quality was the brakes of the system.

84
00:04:59,716 --> 00:05:02,552
If there was a problem,
quality managers stopped the line.

85
00:05:03,594 --> 00:05:05,221
Management had your back.

86
00:05:05,305 --> 00:05:07,682
-Have the guys stand by till we're ready.
-Okay.

87
00:05:07,765 --> 00:05:10,101
-[drill whirs]
-[dramatic music continues]

88
00:05:10,184 --> 00:05:13,813
Then all of a sudden,
that whole atmosphere changed.

89
00:05:16,482 --> 00:05:20,320
So what is it like to be the CEO?
To arrive? What's day one there?

90
00:05:20,403 --> 00:05:22,363
What did you find
when you arrived at Boeing?

91
00:05:22,447 --> 00:05:24,574
The approach was to start
at the beginning.

92
00:05:24,657 --> 00:05:27,702
That the culture needed
a ground-up discussion.

93
00:05:27,785 --> 00:05:30,830
-[continues indistinctly]
-[man 1] The decline of Boeing.

94
00:05:31,331 --> 00:05:35,960
You can trace a lot of it right back
to the hiring of the CEO, Jim McNerney.

95
00:05:36,753 --> 00:05:38,713
He seemed the perfect candidate.

96
00:05:39,213 --> 00:05:44,135
{\an8}He had gone to Yale to do his BA
and gone to Harvard to do his MBA.

97
00:05:44,218 --> 00:05:48,973
Jim McNerney looked like a U.S. senator
from central casting.

98
00:05:51,434 --> 00:05:56,105
{\an8}[man 2] Jim McNerney was
a long-time executive at General Electric

99
00:05:56,189 --> 00:06:02,111
{\an8}and a protégé of arguably the most
influential CEO of the 20th century,

100
00:06:02,195 --> 00:06:03,821
a man named Jack Welch.

101
00:06:03,905 --> 00:06:07,867
{\an8}I'm part, like so many others are,
of an American dream.

102
00:06:08,451 --> 00:06:12,955
{\an8}[Gelles] Jack Welch was
the first CEO of a major American company

103
00:06:13,039 --> 00:06:16,167
to focus on maximizing profits
at all costs.

104
00:06:17,168 --> 00:06:23,383
He was quick to institute outsourcing,
offshoring, mass layoffs.

105
00:06:23,466 --> 00:06:25,551
[Welch] The best thing
you can do to an employee,

106
00:06:25,635 --> 00:06:30,807
as early as you know
they're the bottom ten, let them know.

107
00:06:30,890 --> 00:06:36,062
Famously, Jack Welch at GE
would let the bottom 10% go every year.

108
00:06:36,145 --> 00:06:39,440
Always have people nervous
about their jobs.

109
00:06:40,024 --> 00:06:43,361
[Gelles] With all of these strategies,
Jack Welch turned GE

110
00:06:43,444 --> 00:06:46,864
into the most valuable company
in the world.

111
00:06:47,907 --> 00:06:51,077
The kind of company that
almost every other American corporation

112
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:52,412
looked up to.

113
00:06:54,539 --> 00:06:57,333
And the Boeing board was no exception.

114
00:07:00,253 --> 00:07:03,506
{\an8}I knew I wanted to run something.
I didn't know what it was.

115
00:07:04,549 --> 00:07:08,469
[Gelles] When McNerney takes over,
Boeing goes all in

116
00:07:08,553 --> 00:07:12,682
on the Jack Welch-style
"profits above all else."

117
00:07:12,765 --> 00:07:13,891
[intriguing music playing]

118
00:07:13,975 --> 00:07:18,521
He was also now tasked
with developing a new airplane.

119
00:07:18,604 --> 00:07:20,815
[reporter] <i>The new 787</i>
<i>hasn't been built yet,</i>

120
00:07:20,898 --> 00:07:23,818
<i>but Boeing is so certain</i>
<i>of its creature comforts,</i>

121
00:07:23,901 --> 00:07:25,403
<i>it calls it the Dreamliner.</i>

122
00:07:26,696 --> 00:07:29,407
[Gates] To design and build the 787,

123
00:07:29,490 --> 00:07:32,952
it was going to cost
approximately $10 billion.

124
00:07:33,453 --> 00:07:36,122
But the Boeing board
didn't want to spend that.

125
00:07:36,956 --> 00:07:38,374
[computer beeps]

126
00:07:38,458 --> 00:07:40,126
[printer buzzes]

127
00:07:40,209 --> 00:07:42,462
A source gave me internal documents

128
00:07:42,545 --> 00:07:46,674
showing that Boeing's plan
was to build this plane for $5 billion,

129
00:07:47,175 --> 00:07:49,927
half of what it would normally have cost.

130
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:54,807
[intriguing music continues]

131
00:07:54,891 --> 00:07:57,518
[Meyers] Boeing was always known
for the engineering

132
00:07:57,602 --> 00:07:59,854
and the building of their airplanes.

133
00:08:00,855 --> 00:08:04,734
The real big shift was during the 787.

134
00:08:06,110 --> 00:08:08,863
We found out
that they were going to be outsourcing

135
00:08:08,946 --> 00:08:12,992
major sections of the airplane
to save money.

136
00:08:13,576 --> 00:08:16,454
[Irvin] Mid-body section
was built in Italy.

137
00:08:16,537 --> 00:08:18,915
[Salehpour] Wings and batteries
from Japan.

138
00:08:18,998 --> 00:08:22,835
[Meyers] From South Korea,
wingtips and the aft fuselage parts.

139
00:08:22,919 --> 00:08:25,838
The outsourcing was
almost the entire airplane.

140
00:08:28,591 --> 00:08:32,386
[Mohawk] You knew that was gonna have
a direct effect on safety and quality.

141
00:08:33,804 --> 00:08:37,850
We no longer have the immediate control
about the safety of those parts.

142
00:08:37,934 --> 00:08:41,062
We just have to accept what is given to us
and put them on the airplane.

143
00:08:43,314 --> 00:08:46,234
[Gates] Parts started coming in
from these suppliers…

144
00:08:46,317 --> 00:08:48,736
[solemn music playing]

145
00:08:48,819 --> 00:08:52,907
…and the plan was the plane
would be snapped together in three days.

146
00:08:56,827 --> 00:09:00,414
The first time Boeing joined
the two fuselage barrels together,

147
00:09:00,915 --> 00:09:02,375
somebody sent me a picture.

148
00:09:02,458 --> 00:09:04,794
-[camera clicking]
-[solemn music continues]

149
00:09:06,462 --> 00:09:08,839
There was a huge gap between the two.

150
00:09:11,717 --> 00:09:13,886
{\an8}The problem was
that some of these companies

151
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:16,556
{\an8}couldn't do what Boeing hired them to do.

152
00:09:17,682 --> 00:09:20,768
They couldn't produce
the parts they were supposed to produce.

153
00:09:22,895 --> 00:09:23,854
It went horrible.

154
00:09:25,565 --> 00:09:28,734
Too much of the airplane was outsourced.

155
00:09:31,696 --> 00:09:33,406
Nothing snapped together.

156
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:38,077
And the delays
were just one after the other.

157
00:09:39,370 --> 00:09:43,499
So Boeing wanted to build
a second 787 assembly line

158
00:09:43,583 --> 00:09:46,002
in an effort to increase production.

159
00:09:47,336 --> 00:09:49,922
{\an8}[intriguing music playing]

160
00:09:50,006 --> 00:09:51,924
{\an8}Boeing had started in Seattle

161
00:09:52,008 --> 00:09:54,218
and had never built a plane anywhere else.

162
00:09:54,885 --> 00:09:57,138
[Gelles] This was a place where Boeing had

163
00:09:57,221 --> 00:10:01,976
perhaps the most skilled aviation
manufacturing workforce in the world.

164
00:10:03,644 --> 00:10:06,439
But it was largely unionized.

165
00:10:07,648 --> 00:10:10,359
And McNerney hated unions.

166
00:10:11,319 --> 00:10:13,446
They cost Boeing more money.

167
00:10:13,946 --> 00:10:15,823
And now in 2009, he's saying,

168
00:10:15,906 --> 00:10:20,202
"Well, we're gonna build
a second 787 line in South Carolina."

169
00:10:20,286 --> 00:10:23,914
{\an8}I am always proud to say
we don't have unions in South Carolina

170
00:10:23,998 --> 00:10:26,334
{\an8}because we don't need unions
in South Carolina.

171
00:10:26,417 --> 00:10:28,753
[crowd applauding, cheering]

172
00:10:28,836 --> 00:10:33,633
[Gelles] This was, again,
Boeing leadership making decisions,

173
00:10:33,716 --> 00:10:37,928
not in the name of safety
but in the name of cost savings,

174
00:10:38,012 --> 00:10:40,181
in the name of corporate profits.

175
00:10:40,264 --> 00:10:42,600
{\an8}What have you learned from your engineers

176
00:10:42,683 --> 00:10:46,520
{\an8}about bringing it back
and reconstructing the old Boeing

177
00:10:46,604 --> 00:10:47,855
{\an8}that we all grew up with?

178
00:10:47,938 --> 00:10:50,608
{\an8}Well, we're not going back
to the old Boeing.

179
00:10:52,276 --> 00:10:57,782
-[man] Five, four, three, two, one.
-[crowd] …two, one.

180
00:10:57,865 --> 00:10:59,492
-[man] Cut!
-[crowd cheers]

181
00:10:59,575 --> 00:11:02,328
[Gates] When Boeing set up
the plants in South Carolina,

182
00:11:02,411 --> 00:11:04,455
{\an8}there was no aviation business there.

183
00:11:05,122 --> 00:11:08,125
{\an8}And so, of course, they didn't have
experienced workers.

184
00:11:09,794 --> 00:11:14,215
They constantly were sending experts
from the Seattle area.

185
00:11:14,298 --> 00:11:16,008
[solemn music playing]

186
00:11:16,092 --> 00:11:17,843
John Barnett was one of them.

187
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:23,641
[beeps]

188
00:11:24,767 --> 00:11:26,686
{\an8}When I first started with Boeing,

189
00:11:26,769 --> 00:11:30,523
{\an8}a group of us were standing around
and talking about where you're from.

190
00:11:31,023 --> 00:11:33,150
{\an8}I was telling them
that I'm from Louisiana.

191
00:11:35,111 --> 00:11:37,321
One of my buddies popped off,

192
00:11:37,405 --> 00:11:41,117
"Well, you're just an old swamp dog
from Louisiana, ain't you?" [laughs]

193
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:43,494
You know, from then on, I was "Swampy."

194
00:11:43,577 --> 00:11:44,954
[laughs]

195
00:11:46,414 --> 00:11:50,418
I was a quality manager
in Seattle for 15 years.

196
00:11:52,294 --> 00:11:54,130
I was actually
one of the first quality managers

197
00:11:54,213 --> 00:11:58,634
brought down to Charleston
to help set up the new Dreamliner site.

198
00:12:00,636 --> 00:12:04,640
As a quality manager, it's my job
to make sure the airplane's built right.

199
00:12:06,016 --> 00:12:10,062
John was a solid quality man,
an airplane man.

200
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:12,815
We were both quality managers.

201
00:12:13,315 --> 00:12:14,525
We were good friends.

202
00:12:15,025 --> 00:12:17,695
I used to buy gator tail
by the pound from John

203
00:12:17,778 --> 00:12:19,739
'cause he was a Louisiana boy.

204
00:12:20,823 --> 00:12:22,199
[Barnett] Me being a Southern man,

205
00:12:22,283 --> 00:12:25,453
South Carolina was my opportunity
to get close to my family.

206
00:12:26,495 --> 00:12:28,873
{\an8}We always said he was loud and proud.

207
00:12:28,956 --> 00:12:31,917
{\an8}You knew when he walked in the room
because he was loud. [laughs]

208
00:12:32,001 --> 00:12:33,627
{\an8}-Yeah, Swamp Dog.
-[chuckles]

209
00:12:35,963 --> 00:12:40,050
[man] When I moved to Charleston
and met John Barnett,

210
00:12:40,134 --> 00:12:43,763
I could see that we were
from the same cloth,

211
00:12:43,846 --> 00:12:48,225
{\an8}that quality doesn't take
a back seat to anything.

212
00:12:48,309 --> 00:12:50,853
[intriguing music playing]

213
00:12:50,936 --> 00:12:56,150
John and I really got to know each other
through our recruitment trips.

214
00:12:59,904 --> 00:13:03,866
We would interview
in excess of 100 people in a week.

215
00:13:05,201 --> 00:13:06,702
{\an8}We were told from the beginning

216
00:13:06,786 --> 00:13:09,663
{\an8}they didn't want
the union influence in Charleston.

217
00:13:10,748 --> 00:13:13,751
{\an8}And part of the agreement
that Boeing made with South Carolina

218
00:13:13,834 --> 00:13:16,670
was they would hire
the majority of the employees locally.

219
00:13:17,254 --> 00:13:18,881
We was doing job fairs.

220
00:13:18,964 --> 00:13:21,467
We were hiring people
straight out of college.

221
00:13:23,385 --> 00:13:26,388
Pretty much we got to where
if you knew what a screwdriver was,

222
00:13:26,472 --> 00:13:27,932
you was hired by Boeing.

223
00:13:28,599 --> 00:13:30,434
[man 1] What do you think
about the people here?

224
00:13:30,518 --> 00:13:31,519
[man 2] They fucking suck.

225
00:13:31,602 --> 00:13:33,395
[man 1] They suck. Not everyone of course.

226
00:13:33,479 --> 00:13:36,273
[man 2] Not everyone, but the majority
of them here fucking suck.

227
00:13:36,357 --> 00:13:38,776
[man 1] They're just punching a clock,
that's my opinion.

228
00:13:38,859 --> 00:13:40,152
[man 2] Yeah, it's all they do.

229
00:13:41,612 --> 00:13:43,239
[Barnett] I went to my leadership
and said,

230
00:13:43,322 --> 00:13:45,533
"These people aren't being trained
the way they should."

231
00:13:45,616 --> 00:13:48,285
"Nothing about procedures,
FAA requirements."

232
00:13:48,369 --> 00:13:51,455
"They're just being trained
how to rubber-stamp paperwork."

233
00:13:52,456 --> 00:13:55,084
But my leadership,
they didn't care about training.

234
00:13:55,167 --> 00:13:57,545
They just wanted product
pushed out the door.

235
00:13:59,213 --> 00:14:03,092
Charleston had a workforce
that simply didn't know how to construct

236
00:14:03,175 --> 00:14:06,428
one of the most complicated machines
in the world.

237
00:14:06,512 --> 00:14:09,974
Mistakes started piling up
almost immediately.

238
00:14:13,811 --> 00:14:15,938
[solemn music playing]

239
00:14:16,021 --> 00:14:19,400
[Seitz] I was walking through
the production facility,

240
00:14:19,483 --> 00:14:25,114
and I heard a very loud pounding.

241
00:14:25,197 --> 00:14:27,783
[metallic pounding]

242
00:14:29,285 --> 00:14:34,415
As I got closer, I witnessed
a manager taking a sledgehammer

243
00:14:34,498 --> 00:14:39,295
to pound a pin in a landing gear.

244
00:14:40,087 --> 00:14:43,883
I thought to myself,
I need to record what's going on here.

245
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:49,096
I took that camera
and showed John Barnett.

246
00:14:49,847 --> 00:14:52,016
[solemn music continues]

247
00:14:52,099 --> 00:14:55,227
[Barnett] You don't use a sledgehammer
to put together an airplane.

248
00:14:55,311 --> 00:14:58,314
{\an8}The fuse pin had to be just beat to hell.

249
00:14:59,231 --> 00:15:02,443
{\an8}And actually, back in the '90s,
we had issues with those pins,

250
00:15:02,526 --> 00:15:04,236
{\an8}cracking from fatigue.

251
00:15:04,320 --> 00:15:07,364
[reporter] <i>The Israeli El Al jet</i>
<i>slammed into the building Sunday</i>

252
00:15:07,448 --> 00:15:09,033
<i>after two engines fell off.</i>

253
00:15:09,617 --> 00:15:13,329
<i>Boeing, the company that built</i>
<i>the jumbo jet, is now urging all airlines</i>

254
00:15:13,412 --> 00:15:17,750
<i>to inspect the pins that hold the engines</i>
<i>to the wings of 747s.</i>

255
00:15:18,834 --> 00:15:22,755
[Barnett] There's so many possibilities
for fractures in that, it's unreal.

256
00:15:23,464 --> 00:15:27,426
John said, "Man, you gotta take this
to upper management."

257
00:15:29,011 --> 00:15:33,974
I expected them to reprimand this manager
and make it right.

258
00:15:35,643 --> 00:15:39,355
Instead, I was instructed
to get rid of the video.

259
00:15:40,648 --> 00:15:42,858
[camera beeping]

260
00:15:42,942 --> 00:15:44,902
[solemn music continues]

261
00:15:44,985 --> 00:15:51,450
I was sent off-site to keep me
from investigating what was going on.

262
00:15:52,576 --> 00:15:57,623
It seemed like a way to tell us
production is gonna move on

263
00:15:57,706 --> 00:16:00,209
whether we wanted them to or not.

264
00:16:03,128 --> 00:16:06,715
[McNerney] You've got to end up
being better and reaching higher.

265
00:16:06,799 --> 00:16:09,218
So it's not a matter
of hanging around a hot tub

266
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:11,971
and everybody feeling good
about the conversation.

267
00:16:12,054 --> 00:16:15,891
Uh, there's an element
of push, leadership, uh,

268
00:16:15,975 --> 00:16:19,520
and stretch, uh, provided by the boss.

269
00:16:19,603 --> 00:16:22,231
[tense music playing]

270
00:16:22,314 --> 00:16:25,359
[Gates] Jim McNerney was laser-focused

271
00:16:25,442 --> 00:16:28,988
on meeting the schedule
at the lowest cost.

272
00:16:29,488 --> 00:16:35,869
And so any delays in production
were not to be countenanced.

273
00:16:35,953 --> 00:16:39,415
[man] Here, everybody's being pushed
to meet this fucking schedule,

274
00:16:40,082 --> 00:16:42,126
regardless of quality.

275
00:16:43,293 --> 00:16:46,672
[Irvin] If the airplane was not finished
as quickly as possible,

276
00:16:46,755 --> 00:16:49,174
{\an8}it would hurt the manager's bonuses.

277
00:16:50,843 --> 00:16:53,595
At the time, I was inspecting an airplane.

278
00:16:54,221 --> 00:16:57,599
I saw a hole in the aft body join,

279
00:16:58,350 --> 00:17:01,812
and the fastener that was in there
was not the right size.

280
00:17:02,312 --> 00:17:05,065
I had concerns with what I'd seen.

281
00:17:05,149 --> 00:17:06,525
[tense music builds]

282
00:17:07,109 --> 00:17:09,862
They've lost airplanes
with fasteners like that.

283
00:17:10,988 --> 00:17:13,824
I raised it to my quality manager,

284
00:17:13,907 --> 00:17:16,243
and he said, "Mind your own business."

285
00:17:17,453 --> 00:17:22,249
It was a major ass-chewing for anybody
that would have stopped the process

286
00:17:22,332 --> 00:17:24,668
to be able to fix a defect.

287
00:17:25,294 --> 00:17:27,254
Those people would be in trouble.

288
00:17:28,672 --> 00:17:31,967
[man] It's my pleasure to welcome you
to the certification ceremony

289
00:17:32,051 --> 00:17:34,678
for the Boeing 787 Dreamliner.

290
00:17:34,762 --> 00:17:37,014
[crowd applauding]

291
00:17:37,097 --> 00:17:38,974
[Gates] After the rollout
of the Dreamliner,

292
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:40,684
Boeing was in financial trouble.

293
00:17:40,768 --> 00:17:41,935
[pensive music playing]

294
00:17:42,019 --> 00:17:44,897
And it didn't want to invest
in another all-new design.

295
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:48,442
Because investing in a new plane
is an awful lot of money.

296
00:17:49,234 --> 00:17:52,112
[reporter 1] <i>Boeing finds itself facing</i>
<i>turbulence by a foreign rival</i>

297
00:17:52,196 --> 00:17:53,530
<i>with some very big ambitions.</i>

298
00:17:53,614 --> 00:17:55,991
[reporter 2] <i>Airbus has received</i>
<i>the good-to-go.</i>

299
00:17:56,075 --> 00:17:59,203
<i>The A320neo has the thumbs-up.</i>

300
00:17:59,286 --> 00:18:03,040
[Gates] Then Airbus announced
it would put new fuel-efficient engines

301
00:18:03,123 --> 00:18:04,917
on its most popular jet

302
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,087
that would save
something like 20% of the fuel cost.

303
00:18:08,170 --> 00:18:11,673
[reporter 2] <i>Customers have ordered</i>
<i>over 4,300 of the planes.</i>

304
00:18:11,757 --> 00:18:14,510
[reporter 3] <i>Airbus has simply been</i>
<i>cleaning Boeing's clock.</i>

305
00:18:14,593 --> 00:18:16,637
Jim McNerney freaked out.

306
00:18:17,221 --> 00:18:19,932
They needed to develop a new airplane.

307
00:18:20,724 --> 00:18:24,770
[Gates] Boeing was tending towards
an all-new replacement for the 737.

308
00:18:25,771 --> 00:18:27,689
But that would have taken years.

309
00:18:28,941 --> 00:18:32,361
Jim McNerney made
a really rushed decision.

310
00:18:33,946 --> 00:18:36,156
{\an8}[Gates] McNerney decided,
"Let's just do the simple thing

311
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:39,660
{\an8}and put new fuel-efficient engines
on the existing 737."

312
00:18:41,036 --> 00:18:43,497
{\an8}And that becomes the 737 MAX.

313
00:18:43,580 --> 00:18:44,915
[tense music playing]

314
00:18:44,998 --> 00:18:49,128
[Gates] As soon as they announced this,
it began to have really heavy sales.

315
00:18:49,211 --> 00:18:51,880
[reporter 1] <i>Massive order</i>
<i>that Southwest Airlines will be placing.</i>

316
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:55,801
[reporter 2] <i>Lion Air Group has purchased</i>
<i>50 Boeing 737 MAX aircraft.</i>

317
00:18:55,884 --> 00:18:59,179
We've got an order book
as far as the eye can see.

318
00:18:59,763 --> 00:19:01,765
[reporter 1] <i>Big relief for Boeing.</i>

319
00:19:02,266 --> 00:19:05,060
And that meant profits for shareholders.

320
00:19:05,144 --> 00:19:06,895
-Jim McNerney. [chuckles]
-Thank you.

321
00:19:06,979 --> 00:19:08,772
[Gelles] And a lot of money for the CEO.

322
00:19:08,856 --> 00:19:09,690
Ah!

323
00:19:09,773 --> 00:19:11,150
[tense music continues]

324
00:19:11,233 --> 00:19:14,570
[Gelles] In 2015,
when Jim McNerney stepped down,

325
00:19:14,653 --> 00:19:17,406
he'd made hundreds of millions of dollars

326
00:19:17,489 --> 00:19:21,368
{\an8}and took home
many tens of millions in retirement.

327
00:19:23,287 --> 00:19:25,914
{\an8}I'm sure when he left, he thought,
"Wow, I did a great job."

328
00:19:25,998 --> 00:19:27,416
"Look at the stock price now."

329
00:19:30,169 --> 00:19:32,880
[tense music crescendos, fades]

330
00:19:32,963 --> 00:19:35,632
And because cost-cutting
looked successful,

331
00:19:35,716 --> 00:19:38,760
it was gonna continue to happen
even after he left.

332
00:19:38,844 --> 00:19:40,596
[tense music resumes]

333
00:19:40,679 --> 00:19:42,306
[Gelles] When McNerney stepped down,

334
00:19:42,389 --> 00:19:45,309
{\an8}the board installed
his hand-picked successor,

335
00:19:45,392 --> 00:19:46,977
{\an8}a man named Dennis Muilenburg.

336
00:19:48,896 --> 00:19:52,941
[Gates] Before he took over, Muilenburg
shadowed McNerney for 18 months.

337
00:19:53,025 --> 00:19:56,069
And I think, in that time,
McNerney pointed out to him

338
00:19:56,153 --> 00:20:00,199
all these things he'd been doing
that were so successful financially

339
00:20:00,282 --> 00:20:01,950
and told him, "Keep it up."

340
00:20:02,034 --> 00:20:02,910
So he did.

341
00:20:04,286 --> 00:20:07,039
[man 1] Shares of Boeing
continuing to soar.

342
00:20:07,122 --> 00:20:11,460
[man 2] Were you surprised watching
your stock price move up parabolically?

343
00:20:11,543 --> 00:20:14,963
{\an8}I don't say that I was surprised.
We had promised production ramp-up.

344
00:20:15,047 --> 00:20:16,924
{\an8}We're now delivering on that ramp-up.

345
00:20:17,007 --> 00:20:18,926
{\an8}Which I guess deserves a higher multiple.

346
00:20:19,009 --> 00:20:22,262
{\an8}-That's what I'm thinking.
-We'll talk a lot about commercial but…

347
00:20:22,346 --> 00:20:27,226
{\an8}When Muilenburg came in,
I thought, "Well, nothing changes."

348
00:20:28,227 --> 00:20:29,770
{\an8}And actually, it gets worse.

349
00:20:33,023 --> 00:20:35,275
[ominous music playing]

350
00:20:37,986 --> 00:20:40,322
One day, I was called down
to the Dreamliner.

351
00:20:42,199 --> 00:20:44,368
We found all these metal slivers

352
00:20:44,451 --> 00:20:48,163
on all this electronic wiring
under the floorboard.

353
00:20:51,250 --> 00:20:53,168
As they were installing the floorboards,

354
00:20:53,252 --> 00:20:55,754
it's peeling the threads
off of these fasteners.

355
00:20:57,339 --> 00:21:00,759
Well, over time, this metal sliver's
gonna vibrate down in there,

356
00:21:00,842 --> 00:21:02,427
and it's gonna short wires out.

357
00:21:02,511 --> 00:21:04,012
[electricity zapping]

358
00:21:04,096 --> 00:21:06,974
It's been known that just a little short

359
00:21:07,057 --> 00:21:08,684
will bring down a whole plane.

360
00:21:08,767 --> 00:21:10,102
[whistling]

361
00:21:10,185 --> 00:21:11,812
[ominous music builds]

362
00:21:11,895 --> 00:21:15,107
[pilot] <i>Swissair 111,</i>
<i>we have smoke in the cockpit.</i>

363
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:17,651
<i>Uh, request immediate return.</i>

364
00:21:19,111 --> 00:21:22,155
[Barnett] Swissair Flight 111
had a chafed wire

365
00:21:22,239 --> 00:21:24,616
in an entertainment system,

366
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:26,910
caused a fire
and brought down the whole plane.

367
00:21:26,994 --> 00:21:28,328
Killed everybody on board.

368
00:21:28,829 --> 00:21:31,707
[reporter] <i>We're about 6,000 feet,</i>
<i>hovering above the wreckage.</i>

369
00:21:32,291 --> 00:21:35,794
[Barnett] TWA Flight 800
had a chafed wire in the fuel tanks,

370
00:21:35,877 --> 00:21:37,296
blew it out of the sky.

371
00:21:37,379 --> 00:21:39,089
[solemn music playing]

372
00:21:39,172 --> 00:21:41,466
There's something like 20,000
of these fasteners

373
00:21:41,550 --> 00:21:43,176
on a 787 Dreamliner.

374
00:21:43,677 --> 00:21:46,513
We're looking at
potentially 60,000 slivers

375
00:21:46,596 --> 00:21:48,432
flying around on these wires.

376
00:21:50,809 --> 00:21:52,477
So I brought it up to my boss.

377
00:21:53,478 --> 00:21:57,524
And he said, "Ah, it's gonna cost too much
and take too much time to clean it."

378
00:21:59,651 --> 00:22:02,195
How much is it gonna cost
and how much time is it gonna take

379
00:22:02,279 --> 00:22:05,824
if one of these planes goes down
with 350 people on board?

380
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:09,119
[solemn music continues]

381
00:22:10,620 --> 00:22:12,706
I said, "I will not put
my name on this airplane

382
00:22:12,789 --> 00:22:14,750
saying it's safe and airworthy."

383
00:22:16,501 --> 00:22:18,211
But they didn't want to hear it.

384
00:22:19,880 --> 00:22:22,716
And after that, they moved me
about every four or six months

385
00:22:22,799 --> 00:22:24,092
just to mess with me.

386
00:22:24,176 --> 00:22:26,636
And every time I'd move
and get put in a different area,

387
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:28,263
I'd find some major issue.

388
00:22:28,347 --> 00:22:29,598
[music fades]

389
00:22:31,933 --> 00:22:33,977
[man 1] I just wanted to ask
your opinion, man.

390
00:22:34,061 --> 00:22:36,188
My in-laws are gonna fly on one of these.

391
00:22:36,855 --> 00:22:38,190
Do you think they're safe?

392
00:22:44,279 --> 00:22:46,239
[man 2] They're just
your in-laws. [laughs]

393
00:22:46,323 --> 00:22:47,449
[man 1] Would you fly on one?

394
00:22:47,532 --> 00:22:48,533
[man 2] No.

395
00:22:50,452 --> 00:22:52,037
[man 1] I got a free vacation.

396
00:22:52,120 --> 00:22:52,996
[woman] What's that?

397
00:22:53,080 --> 00:22:55,165
[man 1] You just have to fly on a 787.

398
00:22:55,665 --> 00:22:57,459
-[woman] Shit.
-[man 1] What?

399
00:22:57,542 --> 00:23:00,170
[woman] No, we ain't going
on no vacation on a 787.

400
00:23:00,253 --> 00:23:02,714
You know what be going on on the 787?

401
00:23:03,215 --> 00:23:05,008
[man 1] What happens?

402
00:23:05,092 --> 00:23:06,218
[woman] What don't happen?

403
00:23:06,802 --> 00:23:10,389
[man 3] I wouldn't fly on one
of these planes. Because I see the quality

404
00:23:10,472 --> 00:23:12,808
of the fucking shit
going down around here.

405
00:23:12,891 --> 00:23:15,310
[ominous music playing]

406
00:23:17,312 --> 00:23:19,898
[Barnett] Charleston is the first plant
I've ever heard

407
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:23,610
where the majority of the people
working on the plane will not fly on it.

408
00:23:25,404 --> 00:23:29,741
If your own people do not have
enough confidence to fly on the product,

409
00:23:30,534 --> 00:23:33,495
why are you letting it
go out the door to the flying public?

410
00:23:33,578 --> 00:23:35,789
[jet engines humming]

411
00:23:38,333 --> 00:23:42,421
{\an8}The Charleston plant, we thought,
it's a totally different culture

412
00:23:42,504 --> 00:23:45,298
{\an8}than up here in Seattle.

413
00:23:47,342 --> 00:23:50,971
But in 2015, the alarm bells went off

414
00:23:51,555 --> 00:23:56,893
when I uncovered
that some corroded 787 landing gear axles

415
00:23:56,977 --> 00:24:01,857
that we had previously scrapped,
spray-painted red with the word "scrap,"

416
00:24:01,940 --> 00:24:05,819
wound up being used
back in the Dreamliner production line.

417
00:24:07,571 --> 00:24:10,574
These parts, they were basically rotted.

418
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:15,704
And as I started to research it,
I found that scrap parts

419
00:24:15,787 --> 00:24:19,708
were put back on the airplane
for years and years.

420
00:24:20,208 --> 00:24:23,170
[ominous music intensifies]

421
00:24:26,882 --> 00:24:31,636
We'll never know the scope of how many
of these parts made it to airplanes.

422
00:24:35,432 --> 00:24:39,394
Boeing has delivered
over 1,200 Dreamliners.

423
00:24:39,478 --> 00:24:43,607
The fleet operates
across more than 85 countries

424
00:24:43,690 --> 00:24:45,901
with one billion passengers.

425
00:24:48,904 --> 00:24:51,781
And so we had
a corporate investigation launched.

426
00:24:53,909 --> 00:24:57,954
{\an8}Not one manager was fired
or went to jail or got time off.

427
00:24:59,080 --> 00:25:01,917
It's like it was
just part of the business.

428
00:25:04,503 --> 00:25:07,964
I don't know what it means
for the thousands of planes flying around.

429
00:25:09,007 --> 00:25:11,468
At this point, it's anyone's guess.

430
00:25:14,012 --> 00:25:16,389
[music fades]

431
00:25:17,724 --> 00:25:21,603
{\an8}This new Boeing, the way they treat people
and their quality managers

432
00:25:21,686 --> 00:25:23,730
{\an8}is ruining the entire company.

433
00:25:24,606 --> 00:25:25,941
And that scares me.

434
00:25:26,816 --> 00:25:28,068
Scares me to death.

435
00:25:29,903 --> 00:25:31,780
[Stokes] John would come home worried.

436
00:25:31,863 --> 00:25:36,034
He said, "You know, I just can't believe
that they just don't understand."

437
00:25:36,117 --> 00:25:40,330
I could see later on
that it was starting to really get to him.

438
00:25:43,291 --> 00:25:46,670
[Barnett] I had to seriously think
about going to human resources,

439
00:25:47,170 --> 00:25:51,841
because at Boeing,
when you go to HR, now you're a target.

440
00:25:53,051 --> 00:25:56,638
I knew from past history
and other incidents

441
00:25:56,721 --> 00:25:59,724
that chances are
Boeing's gonna fire you on the spot.

442
00:26:00,225 --> 00:26:03,728
They're gonna confiscate your computer.
They're gonna confiscate your cell phone.

443
00:26:03,812 --> 00:26:06,022
They're gonna confiscate
all your documents.

444
00:26:06,106 --> 00:26:08,817
So now you're a terminated employee

445
00:26:08,900 --> 00:26:12,237
who gets blacklisted
from an entire industry.

446
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:14,698
[solemn music playing]

447
00:26:14,781 --> 00:26:17,075
So I'm scared of what they're gonna do.

448
00:26:20,579 --> 00:26:25,875
But putting the flying public in danger,
this is unacceptable.

449
00:26:27,210 --> 00:26:29,296
I decided I'm going to just fight them.

450
00:26:30,714 --> 00:26:32,424
So I went to human resources.

451
00:26:32,507 --> 00:26:34,884
I told them they're violating procedures.

452
00:26:34,968 --> 00:26:37,387
HR said, "Yep, you're right."

453
00:26:38,430 --> 00:26:40,348
They wouldn't do anything to correct it.

454
00:26:41,224 --> 00:26:43,018
So I filed an ethics complaint.

455
00:26:43,560 --> 00:26:44,477
Nothing.

456
00:26:45,061 --> 00:26:48,565
I wrote a letter to the VP
of Corporate Ethics in Washington, D.C.

457
00:26:49,232 --> 00:26:50,859
Executives didn't do anything.

458
00:26:52,152 --> 00:26:54,237
I filed a complaint with OSHA.

459
00:26:54,988 --> 00:26:59,618
I went to FAA,
the Oversight Enforcement Organization,

460
00:27:00,327 --> 00:27:02,912
to get these issues taken care of.

461
00:27:03,580 --> 00:27:06,249
[solemn music continues]

462
00:27:06,333 --> 00:27:08,710
It's like, does anybody care?

463
00:27:12,464 --> 00:27:15,216
Or is it gonna take an airplane
to go down before some people say,

464
00:27:15,300 --> 00:27:17,636
"Wow, I guess we should have listened."

465
00:27:23,266 --> 00:27:27,020
[woman] I remember waking up
on the Sunday morning.

466
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:30,440
{\an8}I had the two kids next to me,
so I said, you know,

467
00:27:30,523 --> 00:27:32,942
{\an8}it would be nice for him
to… to see a picture of the kids.

468
00:27:33,026 --> 00:27:37,697
{\an8}So I took a little selfie shot
of… of the two kids

469
00:27:37,781 --> 00:27:41,409
saying, "Good morning, Daddy,"
and, you know, lots of emojis.

470
00:27:41,493 --> 00:27:42,494
[text message pops]

471
00:27:44,371 --> 00:27:45,246
[swooshes]

472
00:27:45,747 --> 00:27:48,750
I noticed that the message
hadn't delivered,

473
00:27:49,250 --> 00:27:51,378
which I thought was a little bit unusual.

474
00:27:51,461 --> 00:27:54,881
But I put it down to maybe bad signal
in… in Addis Ababa.

475
00:27:57,300 --> 00:28:01,680
Mick was traveling, doing a lot of work
with the United Nations.

476
00:28:02,847 --> 00:28:06,351
He always had this desire to help people.

477
00:28:06,434 --> 00:28:09,479
[pensive music playing]

478
00:28:10,605 --> 00:28:13,441
{\an8}A number of hours later,
I picked up my phone,

479
00:28:13,942 --> 00:28:16,194
and there was still no reply from him,

480
00:28:16,277 --> 00:28:18,780
but there was a load of missed calls
from… from a friend of mine.

481
00:28:18,863 --> 00:28:20,448
[phone line ringing]

482
00:28:21,574 --> 00:28:25,578
So I rang our friend, he said,
"Naoise, have you seen the news?"

483
00:28:26,079 --> 00:28:27,372
"There's been a plane crash."

484
00:28:28,289 --> 00:28:30,709
And then he said,
"I think Mick is on the plane."

485
00:28:31,960 --> 00:28:34,879
And I said, "No. No."

486
00:28:41,928 --> 00:28:45,849
[reporter] <i>An Ethiopian Airlines flight</i>
<i>has crashed shortly after takeoff,</i>

487
00:28:45,932 --> 00:28:50,812
<i>killing all 157 passengers and crew</i>
<i>thought to be on board.</i>

488
00:28:53,690 --> 00:28:55,817
[Naoise] It's like time stood still.

489
00:29:00,321 --> 00:29:02,991
People don't just disappear
from your life.

490
00:29:03,074 --> 00:29:05,535
They're there one minute,
and then they're not.

491
00:29:07,328 --> 00:29:08,580
But that's what happened.

492
00:29:09,706 --> 00:29:11,291
He wa… he was gone.

493
00:29:17,756 --> 00:29:22,469
I suppose, being an engineer,
that's where my brain kicked in,

494
00:29:22,969 --> 00:29:25,180
and I started to research.

495
00:29:25,680 --> 00:29:27,515
[somber music playing]

496
00:29:27,599 --> 00:29:30,310
[reporter 1] <i>Boeing faces</i>
<i>a lot of safety questions this morning</i>

497
00:29:30,393 --> 00:29:33,605
<i>because this is</i>
<i>the second crash in just five months.</i>

498
00:29:36,191 --> 00:29:39,527
[reporter 2] <i>The Lion Air flight</i>
<i>crashed off the coast of Indonesia</i>

499
00:29:39,611 --> 00:29:42,071
<i>with almost 200 people on board.</i>

500
00:29:42,155 --> 00:29:44,073
[people crying]

501
00:29:46,326 --> 00:29:49,954
[Naoise] I learned this wasn't even
the first MAX crash.

502
00:29:51,289 --> 00:29:56,669
Brand-new planes falling from the sky,
you know, twice.

503
00:29:56,753 --> 00:29:57,962
How could this be?

504
00:29:59,714 --> 00:30:01,341
I wanted answers.

505
00:30:01,841 --> 00:30:03,718
[dramatic music playing]

506
00:30:03,802 --> 00:30:07,472
[reporter 1] <i>An emergency order</i>
<i>grounding all Boeing MAX 8s and 9s.</i>

507
00:30:07,555 --> 00:30:11,684
[reporter 2] <i>The FAA grounded the planes</i>
<i>until their safe use has been confirmed.</i>

508
00:30:11,768 --> 00:30:14,813
[reporter 3] <i>The focus in the two crashes</i>
<i>quickly zeroed in</i>

509
00:30:14,896 --> 00:30:16,981
<i>on a new system called MCAS.</i>

510
00:30:17,065 --> 00:30:20,527
[reporter 4] <i>Powerful MCAS software can</i>
<i>take over the plane's controls in flight.</i>

511
00:30:20,610 --> 00:30:22,654
[reporter 5] <i>That left pilots powerless.</i>

512
00:30:24,531 --> 00:30:27,617
[reporter 6] <i>Boeing withheld</i>
<i>crucial information from the FAA,</i>

513
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:30,745
<i>its customers, and 737 MAX pilots.</i>

514
00:30:31,246 --> 00:30:35,083
[reporter 7] <i>The FAA estimated</i>
<i>that 15 more MAX planes</i>

515
00:30:35,166 --> 00:30:37,585
<i>would crash over the next few decades.</i>

516
00:30:37,669 --> 00:30:41,840
<i>Boeing and its CEO knew</i>
<i>that the flawed system was a safety risk,</i>

517
00:30:41,923 --> 00:30:45,510
{\an8}<i>yet they told the public</i>
<i>MAX was as safe as any airplane</i>

518
00:30:45,593 --> 00:30:47,345
{\an8}<i>that has ever flown the skies.</i>

519
00:30:48,346 --> 00:30:50,807
[Naoise] The more I learned,
the more I realized

520
00:30:50,890 --> 00:30:53,184
that everything was so wrong

521
00:30:53,268 --> 00:30:55,353
and that we needed to push for justice.

522
00:30:55,436 --> 00:30:57,981
[dramatic music continues]

523
00:31:00,275 --> 00:31:05,405
My kids are aware
that their dad got on a plane,

524
00:31:05,488 --> 00:31:07,949
and the plane was made by bad guys.

525
00:31:10,535 --> 00:31:13,371
My son asked me,
"Will we catch the bad guys?"

526
00:31:13,454 --> 00:31:17,166
And I said, "I don't know.
We'll have to wait and see."

527
00:31:18,835 --> 00:31:20,503
[music fades]

528
00:31:25,967 --> 00:31:28,386
[Mohawk] After the 737 MAX crashes,

529
00:31:28,887 --> 00:31:32,974
everyone in our factory
was in a state of shock.

530
00:31:33,975 --> 00:31:36,978
{\an8}We take our work seriously,
especially the work quality,

531
00:31:37,478 --> 00:31:39,606
{\an8}that we're signing off on a good product.

532
00:31:40,440 --> 00:31:43,318
{\an8}And I was devastated.

533
00:31:43,401 --> 00:31:44,277
Um…

534
00:31:46,112 --> 00:31:48,990
[somber music playing]

535
00:31:51,075 --> 00:31:53,077
Can I get a sec? Sorry.

536
00:32:05,757 --> 00:32:07,884
[Meyers] Emotionally, it was a dark cloud,

537
00:32:07,967 --> 00:32:09,260
I think, for everybody.

538
00:32:09,344 --> 00:32:11,930
[somber music continues]

539
00:32:14,140 --> 00:32:15,767
Because what are we doing?

540
00:32:15,850 --> 00:32:17,936
Why are we in this field?

541
00:32:18,019 --> 00:32:19,896
And why do we rush?

542
00:32:22,148 --> 00:32:23,650
[Mohawk] It impacts you.

543
00:32:24,150 --> 00:32:29,072
Every day we spend doing our work,
we're concerned for the flying public, so…

544
00:32:29,656 --> 00:32:32,450
[emotional music playing]

545
00:32:34,619 --> 00:32:37,038
{\an8}[Meyers] We were upset about the families,

546
00:32:37,121 --> 00:32:40,667
{\an8}and especially watching them
with the big pictures of their loved ones.

547
00:32:40,750 --> 00:32:42,877
That's hard for anybody,

548
00:32:42,961 --> 00:32:45,922
but a Boeing employee,
that was very hard to watch.

549
00:32:48,383 --> 00:32:50,218
[woman 1] My daughter was a humanitarian.

550
00:32:50,301 --> 00:32:54,055
She had decided
to dedicate her life to other people.

551
00:32:55,056 --> 00:32:57,684
It was actually her first day of work.

552
00:32:58,685 --> 00:33:02,230
[woman 2] Why did we have to wake up
to get a call

553
00:33:02,939 --> 00:33:06,067
that our two boys
were on a plane that crashed?

554
00:33:06,901 --> 00:33:09,862
[man] Our daughter, Samya,
was headed to East Africa

555
00:33:09,946 --> 00:33:13,533
on her way to start her career
in public health.

556
00:33:15,994 --> 00:33:21,541
What got me the angriest of all
was reading the Boeing lawyers

557
00:33:21,624 --> 00:33:26,254
saying that the families
don't deserve, uh, compensation

558
00:33:26,337 --> 00:33:29,173
for the pain and suffering
that their loved ones went through.

559
00:33:30,925 --> 00:33:33,261
{\an8}They said it happened so fast,

560
00:33:33,344 --> 00:33:35,638
{\an8}the victims didn't know what was going on.

561
00:33:38,307 --> 00:33:41,853
{\an8}I think about
their last six minutes a lot.

562
00:33:42,562 --> 00:33:45,481
{\an8}My wife and my mom-in-law
knew they were going to die.

563
00:33:46,149 --> 00:33:50,737
{\an8}They had to somehow comfort the children
during those final moments,

564
00:33:51,237 --> 00:33:53,322
{\an8}knowing they were all their last.

565
00:33:54,365 --> 00:33:56,200
{\an8}I wish I was there with them.

566
00:33:58,202 --> 00:34:02,707
{\an8}It never leaves me that my family's flesh
is there in Ethiopia,

567
00:34:02,790 --> 00:34:07,545
{\an8}mixed with the soil, the jet fuel,
and pieces of the aircraft.

568
00:34:11,424 --> 00:34:13,843
{\an8}[dramatic sting]

569
00:34:15,386 --> 00:34:20,475
{\an8}In the months after the second crash,
Muilenburg is on the hot seat.

570
00:34:20,558 --> 00:34:24,604
{\an8}Finally, in December of 2019,
Muilenburg is fired.

571
00:34:25,188 --> 00:34:27,190
[solemn music playing]

572
00:34:27,273 --> 00:34:31,402
But even though he had presided
over two devastating crashes,

573
00:34:31,486 --> 00:34:33,571
which had killed hundreds of people,

574
00:34:33,654 --> 00:34:37,492
{\an8}he made about $120 million as CEO

575
00:34:37,575 --> 00:34:42,371
{\an8}and left with a retirement package
worth approximately $60 million.

576
00:34:44,248 --> 00:34:46,084
{\an8}He was immediately replaced

577
00:34:46,167 --> 00:34:49,087
{\an8}by the chairman of the board,
Dave Calhoun.

578
00:34:49,879 --> 00:34:52,381
{\an8}Calhoun was a seasoned executive.

579
00:34:52,465 --> 00:34:55,885
He'd been on the board
for ten years at this point.

580
00:34:56,427 --> 00:34:58,096
[reporter] <i>Boeing entering a new era.</i>

581
00:34:58,179 --> 00:35:01,307
<i>David Calhoun taking over as CEO.</i>
<i>It happens today.</i>

582
00:35:01,390 --> 00:35:04,852
{\an8}At the same time,
there were multiple investigations.

583
00:35:05,937 --> 00:35:08,106
{\an8}The House was very aggressive.

584
00:35:08,189 --> 00:35:12,193
{\an8}We shouldn't have to have tragedies
to change the rules

585
00:35:12,276 --> 00:35:14,028
{\an8}if the rules need to be changed.

586
00:35:14,779 --> 00:35:16,697
We shouldn't have to be here today.

587
00:35:16,781 --> 00:35:20,910
And the DOJ pursued
a possible criminal case.

588
00:35:22,578 --> 00:35:26,207
Nobody knew what legal consequences
Boeing would face.

589
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:30,002
[music fades]

590
00:35:33,756 --> 00:35:37,385
It was two years into the investigations
of the MAX crashes,

591
00:35:38,845 --> 00:35:40,721
I was in my mother-in-law's house,

592
00:35:40,805 --> 00:35:43,224
and we were talking about Mick.

593
00:35:43,307 --> 00:35:45,726
-[indistinct newscast]
-The news was on in the background.

594
00:35:45,810 --> 00:35:47,728
[reporter 1] <i>We have</i>
<i>some breaking news on Boeing.</i>

595
00:35:47,812 --> 00:35:49,564
Suddenly, Boeing is mentioned,

596
00:35:49,647 --> 00:35:52,525
and you kind of stop in your tracks,
and you're listening to it.

597
00:35:52,608 --> 00:35:55,570
<i>Boeing has entered</i>
<i>into a deferred prosecution agreement</i>

598
00:35:55,653 --> 00:35:57,530
<i>regarding the 737 MAX.</i>

599
00:35:57,613 --> 00:36:00,575
And I'm… I'm like,
"What… what the hell? What… what's this?"

600
00:36:00,658 --> 00:36:03,244
You know,
"What's a deferred prosecution agreement?"

601
00:36:03,327 --> 00:36:07,206
<i>This charge will be essentially dismissed</i>
<i>after three years</i>

602
00:36:07,290 --> 00:36:10,751
<i>if Boeing meets all of the stipulations</i>
<i>in this agreement.</i>

603
00:36:10,835 --> 00:36:15,590
<i>This essentially ends</i>
<i>the criminal investigation, if you will,</i>

604
00:36:15,673 --> 00:36:19,468
<i>by the DOJ looking into what Boeing did.</i>

605
00:36:20,178 --> 00:36:24,182
And… and I went, no way, 346 lives lost.

606
00:36:24,265 --> 00:36:26,767
Brand-new planes falling from the sky.

607
00:36:26,851 --> 00:36:29,604
Somebody's got something wrong here.
This can't be true.

608
00:36:29,687 --> 00:36:31,480
[reporter 2] <i>Does that also end</i>
<i>any threats</i>

609
00:36:31,564 --> 00:36:33,316
<i>to individual executives at Boeing?</i>

610
00:36:33,399 --> 00:36:34,859
[reporter 3] <i>Apparently.</i>

611
00:36:34,942 --> 00:36:38,613
We'll get them to pay a fine.
We'll tell them that they're on probation.

612
00:36:38,696 --> 00:36:41,949
If after three years, you've been really,
really good, you get a clean slate.

613
00:36:42,533 --> 00:36:43,993
It's like it never happened.

614
00:36:44,785 --> 00:36:47,747
The CEO should have been prosecuted.

615
00:36:47,830 --> 00:36:50,708
Instead, they wrote that senior management

616
00:36:50,791 --> 00:36:52,877
knew nothing about what was going on.

617
00:36:53,878 --> 00:36:55,922
[reporter 3] <i>There are</i>
<i>a couple of terms within there</i>

618
00:36:56,005 --> 00:36:57,840
<i>in terms of things</i>
<i>that Boeing has to meet.</i>

619
00:36:57,924 --> 00:37:02,428
[Naoise] It is utter disbelief
that somebody could get away

620
00:37:02,511 --> 00:37:05,806
with killing 346 people.

621
00:37:07,141 --> 00:37:09,602
This, it hurts.

622
00:37:10,102 --> 00:37:13,356
[somber music playing]

623
00:37:15,691 --> 00:37:18,486
[Meyers] We were upset
about the plea deal.

624
00:37:19,278 --> 00:37:22,114
It's not justice for these families.

625
00:37:24,367 --> 00:37:27,161
[Irvin] It wasn't nearly severe enough
for Boeing.

626
00:37:27,245 --> 00:37:30,206
It's just checking the box
and… and moving on.

627
00:37:30,289 --> 00:37:33,542
[dramatic music playing]

628
00:37:33,626 --> 00:37:35,169
[Mohawk] The fine's nothing to Boeing.

629
00:37:35,253 --> 00:37:38,965
If there's actually people
that are subject to jail time,

630
00:37:39,048 --> 00:37:40,716
that'll stop their practices.

631
00:37:42,510 --> 00:37:45,638
[Meyers] A lot of these
government agencies think, "Oh my gosh,

632
00:37:45,721 --> 00:37:48,015
you know, if we lead
one of them out in handcuffs,

633
00:37:48,099 --> 00:37:51,102
the whole company…
the house of cards is gonna collapse."

634
00:37:51,185 --> 00:37:53,354
No, you lead them out in handcuffs,

635
00:37:53,437 --> 00:37:55,481
that's gonna make people
start to think twice.

636
00:37:57,900 --> 00:38:01,112
Until you hold people accountable,
they will not change.

637
00:38:03,698 --> 00:38:06,200
[music fades]

638
00:38:07,493 --> 00:38:10,496
[Naoise] I had this feeling
like I needed to do something.

639
00:38:11,497 --> 00:38:13,332
[pensive music playing]

640
00:38:13,416 --> 00:38:15,626
And there was a number of families

641
00:38:15,710 --> 00:38:19,046
that were prepared to stand up and fight.

642
00:38:19,547 --> 00:38:21,007
-[woman 1] <i>Hi, hi.</i>
-[woman 2] <i>Hello.</i>

643
00:38:22,883 --> 00:38:25,386
[Naoise] The only way
we could go after the CEOs

644
00:38:25,469 --> 00:38:28,639
was by proving
that Boeing violated the terms

645
00:38:28,723 --> 00:38:30,683
during their probationary period,

646
00:38:31,642 --> 00:38:35,563
that they had failed to improve
their safety standards.

647
00:38:38,107 --> 00:38:40,192
And we had three years to do that.

648
00:38:42,570 --> 00:38:45,031
[dramatic music playing]

649
00:38:50,411 --> 00:38:53,164
[man] Our families,
we meet every month online.

650
00:38:53,664 --> 00:38:58,127
{\an8}We keep track of
what Boeing's safety failures have been.

651
00:38:58,210 --> 00:38:59,628
There are always more.

652
00:39:00,463 --> 00:39:03,174
[Naoise] There's a reporting procedure
in America.

653
00:39:03,257 --> 00:39:05,426
Every time there's
a problem on an aircraft,

654
00:39:05,509 --> 00:39:08,179
this goes into the system,
and the public can view it.

655
00:39:09,388 --> 00:39:13,309
[Stumo] <i>Yesterday there was a MAX</i>
<i>that declared an emergency in Rome</i>

656
00:39:13,392 --> 00:39:16,562
<i>and another one</i>
<i>that declared an emergency in India.</i>

657
00:39:17,146 --> 00:39:18,981
We were looking for patterns,

658
00:39:19,065 --> 00:39:22,234
and there was a huge amount
with brand-new aircraft.

659
00:39:22,943 --> 00:39:25,446
Electrical problems,
the pressurization problem,

660
00:39:25,529 --> 00:39:29,408
52 bad stabilizer trim motors.
These are critical safety systems.

661
00:39:29,992 --> 00:39:32,495
{\an8}[reporter 1] <i>Boeing's had</i>
<i>production issues with an aft bulkhead.</i>

662
00:39:32,578 --> 00:39:34,789
{\an8}[reporter 2] <i>Electrical problem</i>
<i>with the instrument panel.</i>

663
00:39:34,872 --> 00:39:37,833
{\an8}[reporter 3] <i>One of the right</i>
<i>engine fan blades was fractured.</i>

664
00:39:37,917 --> 00:39:40,086
{\an8}[man 1] <i>All of a sudden,</i>
<i>we hear this loud explosion.</i>

665
00:39:40,169 --> 00:39:43,506
[reporter 4] <i>A hole ripped open mid-flight</i>
<i>in the roof of a Southwest Airlines--</i>

666
00:39:43,589 --> 00:39:47,468
{\an8}[man 2] The FAA has given us a bulletin
saying if you leave the engine anti-ice

667
00:39:47,551 --> 00:39:50,429
{\an8}when you're in clear air
for five minutes or more,

668
00:39:50,513 --> 00:39:52,556
{\an8}the engine could actually come undone.

669
00:39:52,640 --> 00:39:54,100
[dramatic music continues]

670
00:39:54,183 --> 00:39:56,560
I'm flying with a Post-it note
to remind me,

671
00:39:56,644 --> 00:39:58,646
hey, turn that engine anti-ice off

672
00:39:58,729 --> 00:40:00,481
'cause I have no other alert
on the airplane.

673
00:40:03,359 --> 00:40:07,988
And we realized that this wasn't just
a software problem with the MAX aircraft.

674
00:40:08,948 --> 00:40:11,784
There were
enormous manufacturing problems.

675
00:40:13,577 --> 00:40:17,331
We needed to find out what was happening
in the Boeing factories.

676
00:40:21,919 --> 00:40:24,130
[music fades]

677
00:40:26,507 --> 00:40:28,926
[Seitz] John Barnett wanted nothing more

678
00:40:29,009 --> 00:40:32,638
than to see a quality product
go out the door.

679
00:40:33,931 --> 00:40:39,270
{\an8}But it seemed like he was getting
pushback instead of support.

680
00:40:39,770 --> 00:40:41,313
{\an8}John raised up enough issues

681
00:40:41,397 --> 00:40:44,275
{\an8}and raised enough flags
from inside the company

682
00:40:44,358 --> 00:40:47,278
that they eventually decided
to move him to somewhere

683
00:40:47,361 --> 00:40:50,322
where he couldn't have that effect
on the production system.

684
00:40:51,949 --> 00:40:55,953
Seeing what John went through,
it was upsetting.

685
00:40:56,036 --> 00:40:58,456
[solemn music playing]

686
00:41:05,379 --> 00:41:08,466
[Barnett] I had multiple managers
berating me.

687
00:41:09,758 --> 00:41:13,971
{\an8}On my performance management review,
I was downgraded

688
00:41:14,054 --> 00:41:18,476
{\an8}and my pay was docked
for putting quality concerns in writing.

689
00:41:19,602 --> 00:41:22,980
They said flat out they do not want
anything in documentation

690
00:41:23,063 --> 00:41:25,900
so they can maintain culpable deniability.

691
00:41:26,984 --> 00:41:28,569
That's exact words.

692
00:41:31,780 --> 00:41:34,909
Followed that up with what's called
a corrective action plan.

693
00:41:36,118 --> 00:41:38,204
And basically, you're about to get fired.

694
00:41:39,872 --> 00:41:42,291
[solemn music continues]

695
00:41:45,002 --> 00:41:49,256
The stress and the humiliation
and the hostility towards me,

696
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:51,217
it just… it wore me down.

697
00:41:54,637 --> 00:42:00,643
To have 30 years in an industry
where you're looked at as a go-to guy,

698
00:42:00,726 --> 00:42:04,980
and just the next day,
you're just the scum of the organization…

699
00:42:05,940 --> 00:42:07,608
How do you process that?

700
00:42:07,691 --> 00:42:10,236
[solemn music continues]

701
00:42:13,906 --> 00:42:16,325
So I ended up taking a retirement.

702
00:42:19,620 --> 00:42:20,955
[music fades]

703
00:42:21,038 --> 00:42:23,040
[birds chirping]

704
00:42:25,960 --> 00:42:31,799
But after I left,
I knew issues continued to exist.

705
00:42:33,425 --> 00:42:35,928
That weighs heavy on my heart.

706
00:42:38,931 --> 00:42:43,602
Maybe I should have went to the president
of Boeing instead of just the VP.

707
00:42:43,686 --> 00:42:46,772
I mean, you know, you can't help
but think about those things.

708
00:42:46,855 --> 00:42:48,983
You know,
what could I have done different?

709
00:42:52,403 --> 00:42:55,489
And I'm sitting there, I'm thinking,
"What do I do now?"

710
00:42:57,032 --> 00:43:00,244
And it was a choice
of either "sit down, shut up"

711
00:43:00,327 --> 00:43:01,662
or go outside of Boeing.

712
00:43:01,745 --> 00:43:05,165
[contemplative music playing]

713
00:43:07,835 --> 00:43:10,379
We're talking airplanes,
we're talking people's lives,

714
00:43:10,462 --> 00:43:12,089
we're talking the flying public.

715
00:43:13,173 --> 00:43:15,593
I can't sit back and let that go.

716
00:43:15,676 --> 00:43:17,720
[music continues]

717
00:43:23,350 --> 00:43:25,144
And I wasn't gonna sit down, shut up.

718
00:43:27,354 --> 00:43:29,940
[reporter] <i>John Barnett says</i>
<i>he spent 32 years with Boeing,</i>

719
00:43:30,024 --> 00:43:32,151
<i>and he's blowing the whistle</i>
<i>on where he works.</i>

720
00:43:32,234 --> 00:43:35,738
It's just all been about schedule
and hurry up and just get it done,

721
00:43:35,821 --> 00:43:37,781
push the planes out,
we're behind schedule.

722
00:43:37,865 --> 00:43:40,951
I know what's under the skin,
and it's scary.

723
00:43:42,786 --> 00:43:45,497
<i>I just think it's critically important</i>

724
00:43:45,581 --> 00:43:49,084
<i>that the activities going on within Boeing</i>

725
00:43:49,168 --> 00:43:52,338
<i>are made aware to the people</i>
<i>whose lives could be affected.</i>

726
00:43:52,921 --> 00:43:55,215
<i>You know, I mean, look at the 737 MAX.</i>

727
00:43:56,383 --> 00:43:59,470
[sighs] <i>Those lives and those families.</i>

728
00:44:01,597 --> 00:44:03,140
<i>That's why I keep telling my story.</i>

729
00:44:03,223 --> 00:44:05,643
<i>Somebody's got to step in</i>
<i>and get it addressed.</i>

730
00:44:09,938 --> 00:44:11,940
[Naoise] As we continued to research,

731
00:44:13,359 --> 00:44:16,487
we became aware that
there was a small group of workers

732
00:44:16,570 --> 00:44:20,658
that had come forward to say
that there were manufacturing problems

733
00:44:20,741 --> 00:44:22,451
at the Boeing factory,

734
00:44:22,534 --> 00:44:24,161
that there was total chaos.

735
00:44:24,244 --> 00:44:27,164
And some of them
have reached out to us personally.

736
00:44:30,084 --> 00:44:34,213
{\an8}We've had the honor of working
with many of you for several years now.

737
00:44:34,296 --> 00:44:35,339
And there's been a lot of…

738
00:44:35,422 --> 00:44:38,717
[Naoise] We started to be in contact a lot
with whistleblowers.

739
00:44:39,301 --> 00:44:41,637
We had kind of formed a group
at this stage.

740
00:44:41,720 --> 00:44:42,846
[indistinct chatter]

741
00:44:42,930 --> 00:44:47,935
There was a number of meetings with them,
some in person, then some online.

742
00:44:50,145 --> 00:44:52,898
We couldn't just sit back and do nothing.

743
00:44:52,981 --> 00:44:56,652
Because as families, we need to feel
that our loved ones mattered.

744
00:44:58,028 --> 00:45:01,907
But also because real justice
and accountability

745
00:45:01,990 --> 00:45:05,077
can prevent something from happening
in the future.

746
00:45:05,160 --> 00:45:07,204
[anticipatory music playing]

747
00:45:09,081 --> 00:45:12,084
[woman] How long do you think
we're gonna be there again?

748
00:45:12,167 --> 00:45:13,293
-In the meeting?
-Yeah.

749
00:45:13,377 --> 00:45:15,003
Oh, another three, four hours.

750
00:45:17,464 --> 00:45:21,093
[Naoise] And then we started having
these meetings with the FAA.

751
00:45:21,677 --> 00:45:24,805
[Stumo] The FAA is
the Federal Aviation Administration.

752
00:45:24,888 --> 00:45:26,807
Their job is to make sure

753
00:45:26,890 --> 00:45:30,394
there's the safe design
and manufacture of aircraft.

754
00:45:30,477 --> 00:45:34,189
It has a large part of its duty
to monitor and inspect Boeing.

755
00:45:36,984 --> 00:45:39,945
[Naoise] This deferred
prosecution agreement was going to expire

756
00:45:40,028 --> 00:45:41,822
in about two years.

757
00:45:41,905 --> 00:45:46,493
So every week we informed the FAA that
there was still an awful lot of problems.

758
00:45:46,577 --> 00:45:50,873
[Stumo] We're seeing continuing problems
with brand-new aircraft

759
00:45:50,956 --> 00:45:52,958
that you're not seeing
with older aircraft.

760
00:45:53,041 --> 00:45:55,544
It's just lunacy that it's not fixed.

761
00:45:57,129 --> 00:45:59,339
{\an8}[Naoise] And what they said
at the time was,

762
00:45:59,423 --> 00:46:01,175
{\an8}"You don't need to worry about it."

763
00:46:01,800 --> 00:46:05,763
"Boeing have changed their ways,
and they're a fantastic company now."

764
00:46:07,264 --> 00:46:08,515
[music fades]

765
00:46:08,599 --> 00:46:12,561
We're gonna share all issues
so that when things do go a little wrong,

766
00:46:12,644 --> 00:46:16,482
we can get ahead of them with
all of our employees and with the public.

767
00:46:16,565 --> 00:46:18,567
[mellow music playing]

768
00:46:20,194 --> 00:46:24,114
Many people had hopes that
Calhoun would turn the company around.

769
00:46:24,198 --> 00:46:26,408
{\an8}CEO of Boeing, he mentioned the guidance…

770
00:46:26,492 --> 00:46:28,243
{\an8}[Mohawk] We need somebody
to make a change.

771
00:46:28,327 --> 00:46:31,121
{\an8}Hopefully, he's the person that will make
the change within our company.

772
00:46:32,498 --> 00:46:36,210
[Meyers] Dave Calhoun committed
to cleaning up processes.

773
00:46:36,835 --> 00:46:38,837
[man] It reorganized
its engineering organization.

774
00:46:38,921 --> 00:46:40,589
It created a chief safety officer.

775
00:46:40,672 --> 00:46:43,050
{\an8}All these different tactical positions

776
00:46:43,133 --> 00:46:46,637
{\an8}that allowed them to… to make sure
that this doesn't happen again.

777
00:46:48,680 --> 00:46:50,891
[Mohawk] They started to create
some kind of system

778
00:46:50,974 --> 00:46:53,560
where leadership listened
to what was going on on the floor.

779
00:46:54,144 --> 00:46:57,064
[woman] <i>Welcome</i>
<i>to the Boeing Company's conference call.</i>

780
00:46:57,147 --> 00:47:00,067
[Calhoun] <i>We will encourage</i>
<i>and reward employees for speaking up</i>

781
00:47:00,150 --> 00:47:03,028
<i>to slow things down</i>
<i>if that's what's needed.</i>

782
00:47:04,863 --> 00:47:09,535
The Speak Up program
was going to make it easy for employees

783
00:47:09,618 --> 00:47:13,539
to identify ethical problems,
bad behaviors,

784
00:47:13,622 --> 00:47:17,209
and safety concerns to get quicker action.

785
00:47:17,292 --> 00:47:19,711
Transparency is what we lost for a moment,

786
00:47:19,795 --> 00:47:21,255
and it's something we have to regain

787
00:47:21,338 --> 00:47:24,675
because it speaks to the trust
that the world has in us.

788
00:47:24,758 --> 00:47:26,260
[signal beeping]

789
00:47:29,179 --> 00:47:33,809
Breaking news.
Boeing's 737 MAX is allowed to fly again.

790
00:47:33,892 --> 00:47:36,478
The FAA just lifted the grounding
this morning.

791
00:47:36,562 --> 00:47:39,648
[reporter 1] <i>The company has worked</i>
<i>to update the software, computers,</i>

792
00:47:39,731 --> 00:47:41,692
<i>and pilot training for the MAX.</i>

793
00:47:41,775 --> 00:47:44,361
[man] <i>You are flying on a Boeing 737 MAX.</i>

794
00:47:44,444 --> 00:47:46,363
[reporter 2] <i>In this</i>
<i>American Airlines cabin,</i>

795
00:47:46,446 --> 00:47:50,325
<i>applause for a new chapter</i>
<i>in 737 MAX safety</i>

796
00:47:50,409 --> 00:47:53,871
<i>as the first domestic departure</i>
<i>roared off the runway.</i>

797
00:47:53,954 --> 00:47:56,957
[Calhoun] I'm very proud of our team,
and I'm very proud of that airplane.

798
00:47:57,040 --> 00:47:59,585
It is a, uh, remarkable, uh, machine,

799
00:47:59,668 --> 00:48:01,920
and… and as safe as anything…
anything in the air.

800
00:48:02,004 --> 00:48:04,172
[engine roaring]

801
00:48:04,256 --> 00:48:09,469
[Gates] After the planes were ungrounded,
I was convinced that the MAX was safe.

802
00:48:10,095 --> 00:48:11,555
It was safe to fly.

803
00:48:13,473 --> 00:48:15,726
[reporter 1] <i>Big boost for Boeing</i>
<i>and its 737 MAX.</i>

804
00:48:15,809 --> 00:48:19,354
[reporter 2] <i>A multi-billion dollar order</i>
<i>for Southwest Airlines.</i>

805
00:48:19,438 --> 00:48:22,024
[reporter 3] <i>Ryanair ordered 75 MAXs.</i>

806
00:48:22,107 --> 00:48:24,610
[reporter 4] <i>Looking to increase</i>
<i>production of the 737 MAX.</i>

807
00:48:24,693 --> 00:48:28,030
[reporter 5] <i>The rent and flight line</i>
<i>tonight is very busy.</i>

808
00:48:28,113 --> 00:48:30,198
[reporter 3] <i>Ryanair's CEO says the MAX</i>

809
00:48:30,282 --> 00:48:34,286
<i>is now the safest plane</i>
<i>in aviation history.</i>

810
00:48:37,080 --> 00:48:41,877
Once the MAX was back in the air,
the whole atmosphere changed.

811
00:48:41,960 --> 00:48:43,754
[mysterious music playing]

812
00:48:43,837 --> 00:48:46,173
[Mohawk] Boeing went back
to business as usual.

813
00:48:46,256 --> 00:48:47,549
It's time to make money again.

814
00:48:47,633 --> 00:48:49,885
Let's push these airplanes out
and make that money.

815
00:48:51,470 --> 00:48:53,013
Work exploded.

816
00:48:53,096 --> 00:48:56,141
I was now responsible for, like,
ten times the amount of airplanes

817
00:48:56,224 --> 00:48:57,517
I'm usually responsible for.

818
00:48:58,644 --> 00:49:01,855
[Meyers] It was a schedule-driven,
chaotic rush again.

819
00:49:02,564 --> 00:49:05,192
We were having a lot of problems.

820
00:49:05,901 --> 00:49:10,322
We had counted about 50,000 parts
that bypassed quality

821
00:49:10,405 --> 00:49:12,366
that had escaped to the airplane.

822
00:49:13,325 --> 00:49:15,452
A lot of flight-critical parts too.

823
00:49:17,204 --> 00:49:19,873
So I reported this
through the Speak Up program.

824
00:49:21,291 --> 00:49:24,753
There are so many
of my nonconformance records still open.

825
00:49:24,836 --> 00:49:27,381
And that's usually because
the part has gone missing.

826
00:49:27,464 --> 00:49:30,759
Head of our material review board
came up with the decision

827
00:49:30,842 --> 00:49:33,261
to go ahead and delete
those nonconformance records.

828
00:49:35,681 --> 00:49:37,057
That was a criminal activity.

829
00:49:37,140 --> 00:49:40,018
So the first thing I did was
I went through the Speak Up system.

830
00:49:41,186 --> 00:49:42,270
[mouse clicks]

831
00:49:42,354 --> 00:49:46,483
We're seeing a lot of defects
in these joint areas

832
00:49:47,150 --> 00:49:49,236
where the parts are coming together.

833
00:49:50,362 --> 00:49:52,906
I brought that up in a Speak Up.

834
00:49:53,699 --> 00:49:58,245
But I don't think a whistleblower
calls Speak Up more than once.

835
00:50:00,205 --> 00:50:04,459
Because when we did,
we were immediately retaliated against.

836
00:50:05,168 --> 00:50:07,170
[train rattling]

837
00:50:11,633 --> 00:50:16,221
{\an8}My boss came to my desk
and chewed me out, and he said,

838
00:50:16,304 --> 00:50:18,932
{\an8}"I would have killed
someone who said what you said."

839
00:50:19,016 --> 00:50:20,684
{\an8}[tense music playing]

840
00:50:20,767 --> 00:50:21,810
What did I say?

841
00:50:21,893 --> 00:50:25,772
I said, we need to consider
engineering fundamentals.

842
00:50:27,399 --> 00:50:29,943
{\an8}[Mohawk] They were trying to work me
through a corrective action,

843
00:50:30,027 --> 00:50:32,904
{\an8}saying that I wasn't listening
to leadership doing my job.

844
00:50:34,614 --> 00:50:36,241
[Meyers] The ethics advisor called me

845
00:50:36,908 --> 00:50:39,661
and said, "Merle, yeah,
you're going to be singled out

846
00:50:39,745 --> 00:50:41,455
and made an example of."

847
00:50:42,873 --> 00:50:47,627
My senior manager said,
"Here's 12 things that you're doing wrong,

848
00:50:47,711 --> 00:50:50,255
and you have to correct these
in 30 days, or I'll fire you."

849
00:50:54,092 --> 00:50:57,971
"I started keeping documentation
about safety concerns."

850
00:51:01,058 --> 00:51:05,437
I was thinking,
"I don't want to call the press."

851
00:51:07,397 --> 00:51:09,733
Because we've seen with whistleblowing,

852
00:51:09,816 --> 00:51:12,694
you lose your job,
you lose your community.

853
00:51:14,654 --> 00:51:17,699
[Mohawk] I was whistleblowing internally
for a couple years,

854
00:51:17,783 --> 00:51:19,951
but I never wanted to go public.

855
00:51:21,161 --> 00:51:23,455
Because I'm just, at the end of the day,
an employee.

856
00:51:24,039 --> 00:51:25,457
I would have been crucified.

857
00:51:28,460 --> 00:51:32,756
[Salehpour] A lot of my coworkers
were really scared to come forward,

858
00:51:32,839 --> 00:51:34,091
even within the company.

859
00:51:36,259 --> 00:51:41,473
But when I see a problematic area
that could cause loss of life,

860
00:51:41,556 --> 00:51:44,267
I'm gonna speak up until my face is blue.

861
00:51:45,435 --> 00:51:47,479
So I kept on following it.

862
00:51:48,605 --> 00:51:52,818
And they finally just kicked me
out of the Dreamliner program.

863
00:51:56,404 --> 00:51:58,949
Then my tire was slashed.

864
00:51:59,991 --> 00:52:04,913
I didn't know who did it,
but I think that was a warning call.

865
00:52:05,539 --> 00:52:08,834
Things are a little more serious
than what you want to be.

866
00:52:12,379 --> 00:52:14,047
It threw me off.

867
00:52:14,131 --> 00:52:19,928
I was waking up with, uh, nightmares
three o'clock every morning.

868
00:52:20,011 --> 00:52:25,183
And I was talking to three different
psychologists at the time,

869
00:52:26,059 --> 00:52:28,603
to just be able to cope with it.

870
00:52:29,396 --> 00:52:31,398
[somber music playing]

871
00:52:38,947 --> 00:52:41,158
[Irvin] There's a lot of lives at stake.

872
00:52:44,077 --> 00:52:48,081
I would have nightmares of airplanes
crashing very close to me.

873
00:52:48,165 --> 00:52:53,461
I've had airplanes crash so close to me,
I can feel the heat from them in my sleep.

874
00:52:56,673 --> 00:52:58,091
[Mohawk] That keeps me up at night.

875
00:52:58,175 --> 00:53:01,094
I hope there's
not another MAX airplane loss,

876
00:53:01,178 --> 00:53:02,929
but it does keep me up at night.

877
00:53:06,349 --> 00:53:11,938
Just losing battle after battle,
and betrayed over and over again.

878
00:53:12,814 --> 00:53:14,858
It just is gonna weigh you down.

879
00:53:16,776 --> 00:53:18,111
I felt helpless.

880
00:53:21,656 --> 00:53:23,867
[Seitz] The workers
would be more than happy

881
00:53:23,950 --> 00:53:25,952
to speak out to the public,

882
00:53:26,870 --> 00:53:29,456
but they're in fear of losing their jobs.

883
00:53:30,415 --> 00:53:33,627
They have years to work.

884
00:53:34,169 --> 00:53:37,964
They can't afford to be fired
from their positions.

885
00:53:39,299 --> 00:53:42,886
There's definitely a culture of fear.

886
00:53:42,969 --> 00:53:44,971
[crickets chirping]

887
00:53:45,055 --> 00:53:47,349
[boat engine rumbling]

888
00:53:53,313 --> 00:53:57,234
{\an8}After John left Boeing,
he kept seeing all these problems,

889
00:53:57,317 --> 00:53:58,568
{\an8}and it really weighed on him.

890
00:53:59,903 --> 00:54:02,030
So we filed the case.

891
00:54:02,948 --> 00:54:06,576
{\an8}John Barnett's case against Boeing
involved exposure

892
00:54:06,660 --> 00:54:08,328
{\an8}to a hostile work environment,

893
00:54:09,037 --> 00:54:13,458
with allegations of retaliation
by Boeing against him.

894
00:54:14,376 --> 00:54:15,961
He had been gaslighted

895
00:54:16,044 --> 00:54:19,589
for years and years and years and years
when he was there.

896
00:54:19,673 --> 00:54:22,592
Mr. Barnett, you testified that,
what did he say,

897
00:54:22,676 --> 00:54:24,761
something about he was gonna break you?

898
00:54:24,844 --> 00:54:27,180
-He was gonna push me until I broke.
-[Boeing lawyer] Okay.

899
00:54:27,264 --> 00:54:29,474
Was… Was he joking when he said that?

900
00:54:29,557 --> 00:54:30,767
Oh, absolutely not.

901
00:54:31,935 --> 00:54:33,478
[Turkewitz] We filed a complaint,

902
00:54:33,561 --> 00:54:37,607
and we had to compel Boeing
to produce documents.

903
00:54:38,400 --> 00:54:41,820
It took forever
to get responses from Boeing.

904
00:54:44,197 --> 00:54:47,617
Whistleblower cases
are very complicated cases.

905
00:54:48,576 --> 00:54:53,623
Oftentimes, when whistleblowers
go public, when they're terminated,

906
00:54:53,707 --> 00:54:58,586
the employer takes possession
of their laptops or their phones.

907
00:54:59,587 --> 00:55:01,923
That means they have no evidence
to back up.

908
00:55:02,007 --> 00:55:03,550
It's just he said, she said.

909
00:55:03,633 --> 00:55:05,218
[pensive music playing]

910
00:55:06,344 --> 00:55:10,557
[Turkewitz] What really set
John's situation apart from others

911
00:55:10,640 --> 00:55:13,310
is that John had actually
documented everything.

912
00:55:15,437 --> 00:55:18,148
And he said that someday in the future,

913
00:55:18,231 --> 00:55:20,567
there was gonna be
a reckoning with Boeing,

914
00:55:20,650 --> 00:55:24,321
and he was gonna have the proof
to demonstrate where they failed.

915
00:55:25,196 --> 00:55:28,783
[Boeing lawyer] Let's talk about
the oxygen investigation.

916
00:55:28,867 --> 00:55:35,123
Around July 2016, there was an issue
with defective oxygen canisters.

917
00:55:35,206 --> 00:55:36,708
-Is that correct?
-Right.

918
00:55:38,126 --> 00:55:42,088
[Boeing lawyer] You discovered
that some of the oxygen squibs

919
00:55:42,172 --> 00:55:44,466
were not properly dispensing oxygen.

920
00:55:45,383 --> 00:55:48,094
[Barnett] We had about 15%
that didn't initiate.

921
00:55:49,179 --> 00:55:52,515
[Boeing lawyer] You had just performed
kind of informal tests

922
00:55:52,599 --> 00:55:55,769
and had come up
with this 15% failure rate, correct?

923
00:55:56,353 --> 00:55:59,397
No, it really wasn't an informal test.

924
00:56:00,774 --> 00:56:03,735
-I said, "We need to take a closer look."
-[Boeing lawyer] Mm-hmm.

925
00:56:03,818 --> 00:56:06,446
[Barnett] So I actually pulled
fresh squibs out of stores,

926
00:56:06,529 --> 00:56:08,865
still in the packages,

927
00:56:08,948 --> 00:56:11,201
and we sent that control group.

928
00:56:13,745 --> 00:56:16,164
The failure rate actually went up to 25%.

929
00:56:16,247 --> 00:56:17,624
That set off all kinds of alarms,

930
00:56:17,707 --> 00:56:19,626
and I was like, "Okay,
this is a serious issue."

931
00:56:21,503 --> 00:56:25,298
{\an8}Say you're flying at 40,000 feet,
and for some reason,

932
00:56:25,382 --> 00:56:28,551
{\an8}you have a massive decompression,
and the oxygen masks drop.

933
00:56:28,635 --> 00:56:29,594
[alert dinging]

934
00:56:31,638 --> 00:56:34,265
{\an8}You have 20 seconds to get your mask on

935
00:56:34,349 --> 00:56:36,684
{\an8}before it starts getting into
lack of oxygen,

936
00:56:36,768 --> 00:56:40,563
{\an8}and it'll actually create brain damage
and, in some cases, even death.

937
00:56:40,647 --> 00:56:41,940
{\an8}[clock beeping]

938
00:56:42,023 --> 00:56:44,192
{\an8}Imagine you're not getting oxygen.

939
00:56:46,027 --> 00:56:48,196
{\an8}Say you're sitting there with your kids.

940
00:56:48,279 --> 00:56:50,115
{\an8}Who gets the oxygen? Who don't?

941
00:56:50,198 --> 00:56:52,200
{\an8}-[pensive music playing]
-[zapping]

942
00:56:56,538 --> 00:56:59,082
[Boeing lawyer] During the course
of you documenting

943
00:56:59,165 --> 00:57:01,042
the issue with the oxygen squibs,

944
00:57:01,126 --> 00:57:04,629
no one criticized you
for documenting that issue, correct?

945
00:57:04,712 --> 00:57:06,673
Actually, we do have an email.

946
00:57:07,590 --> 00:57:08,800
Pretty nasty.

947
00:57:10,218 --> 00:57:12,595
He's like, just stop the madness.

948
00:57:12,679 --> 00:57:14,055
That's the kind of attitude he had.

949
00:57:14,139 --> 00:57:17,642
[Boeing lawyer] Mm-hmm. We didn't see that
in any of the emails we just looked at.

950
00:57:17,725 --> 00:57:20,478
-Not these, but you have my copies, yes.
-[Boeing lawyer] Okay. Okay.

951
00:57:22,856 --> 00:57:26,484
[Turkewitz] John used to say
that it takes 8, 10, 12 years

952
00:57:26,568 --> 00:57:28,528
for those defects to manifest.

953
00:57:29,237 --> 00:57:31,739
So he was really concerned
about these problems,

954
00:57:31,823 --> 00:57:35,368
that they would manifest
down the road and cause a disaster.

955
00:57:42,584 --> 00:57:46,296
[Naoise] The three-year probation
from the Department of Justice

956
00:57:46,379 --> 00:57:48,256
was going to expire in a month.

957
00:57:48,840 --> 00:57:51,050
{\an8}That was kind of a mental process,

958
00:57:51,134 --> 00:57:54,345
{\an8}a psychological process
we were all going through.

959
00:57:54,429 --> 00:57:56,598
[somber music playing]

960
00:57:56,681 --> 00:58:00,101
The meetings had gone on
for months and months.

961
00:58:01,936 --> 00:58:06,900
I even flew to Washington to sit down
with the Department of Justice

962
00:58:07,525 --> 00:58:11,112
to explain our concerns
that nothing had changed

963
00:58:11,196 --> 00:58:14,866
in the Boeing culture
and in the Boeing factories.

964
00:58:16,534 --> 00:58:20,038
We sat down with
the head of the fraud section, Glenn Leon.

965
00:58:21,080 --> 00:58:23,625
Then he started to read from a script.

966
00:58:24,125 --> 00:58:28,004
And if we said, "Hang on a sec,
what about what we have to say?"

967
00:58:28,588 --> 00:58:30,256
he might huff and puff a bit.

968
00:58:30,882 --> 00:58:33,468
And then he carried on
as though we hadn't said anything.

969
00:58:35,011 --> 00:58:37,931
It very much seemed
like the Department of Justice

970
00:58:38,014 --> 00:58:40,350
was trying to protect Boeing.

971
00:58:40,433 --> 00:58:42,101
[contemplative music playing]

972
00:58:42,185 --> 00:58:44,062
[cameras clicking]

973
00:58:44,145 --> 00:58:47,357
We go to these courtrooms,
and the DOJ and the Boeing lawyers

974
00:58:47,440 --> 00:58:50,527
are on one side of the courtroom
talking to each other,

975
00:58:50,610 --> 00:58:52,070
and we're on the other side.

976
00:58:52,153 --> 00:58:53,738
It's like we're the criminals.

977
00:58:54,864 --> 00:58:58,117
[Naoise] When you see
the number of lawyers that have passed

978
00:58:58,201 --> 00:59:00,328
from Boeing into the Department of Justice

979
00:59:00,411 --> 00:59:04,082
and from the Department of Justice
into the Boeing law firm,

980
00:59:04,165 --> 00:59:07,168
it is complete political entanglement.

981
00:59:12,048 --> 00:59:15,760
Now the deferred prosecution agreement
was about to expire.

982
00:59:17,512 --> 00:59:19,722
There was no hope. There was no justice.

983
00:59:19,806 --> 00:59:21,683
There was never
going to be accountability.

984
00:59:21,766 --> 00:59:23,017
Forget it. Go home.

985
00:59:25,353 --> 00:59:29,941
[man 1] It doesn't seem that the DOJ is
interested in getting the criminals out.

986
00:59:30,024 --> 00:59:35,196
They're interested in getting this case
behind them any way they can.

987
00:59:35,280 --> 00:59:38,658
And they're gonna allow the criminals
to stay in and do what.

988
00:59:38,741 --> 00:59:41,494
And a lesson will never be learned
by anybody.

989
00:59:41,578 --> 00:59:42,870
[man 2] Right now, we are showing

990
00:59:42,954 --> 00:59:47,667
that there are countless issues
with production quality matters.

991
00:59:47,750 --> 00:59:49,586
When is the next crash gonna be?

992
00:59:49,669 --> 00:59:51,296
I hope it doesn't happen.

993
00:59:51,379 --> 00:59:53,631
But this is what we're dealing with.

994
01:00:00,346 --> 01:00:02,265
[pensive music playing]

995
01:00:07,562 --> 01:00:11,232
[pilot over radio] <i>Ground, Alaska 1282,</i>
<i>push up Charlie 7 North Yankee.</i>

996
01:00:11,316 --> 01:00:13,860
[air traffic controller]
<i>Pushback's approved, 2-8 Left.</i>

997
01:00:14,694 --> 01:00:19,490
[passenger 1] It was a typical start-up,
taxi, takeoff.

998
01:00:20,742 --> 01:00:23,828
[air traffic controller] <i>Alaska 1282.</i>
<i>Runway 2-8 Left. Cleared to take off.</i>

999
01:00:24,579 --> 01:00:26,372
[pilot] <i>Good day, Alaska 1282.</i>

1000
01:00:27,790 --> 01:00:31,210
[passenger 2] My daughter pulled out
all sorts of sketching materials

1001
01:00:31,294 --> 01:00:33,046
as the flight took off.

1002
01:00:34,714 --> 01:00:36,966
[passenger 1] My son
was sitting on my lap.

1003
01:00:37,050 --> 01:00:39,218
My 3-year-old was in the middle.

1004
01:00:39,302 --> 01:00:41,596
And then my 5-year-old
was in the window seat.

1005
01:00:44,974 --> 01:00:46,643
[passenger 2] We're climbing.

1006
01:00:46,726 --> 01:00:48,311
I remember looking over

1007
01:00:48,394 --> 01:00:50,938
and just kind of wanting to see
how my family was doing.

1008
01:00:51,731 --> 01:00:54,942
[pilot] <i>Seattle, Alaska 1282,</i>
<i>11,000 climbing 15,000.</i>

1009
01:00:55,026 --> 01:00:57,028
[air traffic controller] <i>Alaska 1282,</i>
<i>Seattle Center,</i>

1010
01:00:57,111 --> 01:00:59,030
<i>climb and maintain level of 2-3-0.</i>

1011
01:01:01,199 --> 01:01:02,909
[high-pitched whistling]

1012
01:01:02,992 --> 01:01:08,748
[passenger 2] There was a high-pitched
whistling sound and then a boom.

1013
01:01:08,831 --> 01:01:09,999
[loud bang]

1014
01:01:10,708 --> 01:01:11,542
[alarms blaring]

1015
01:01:11,626 --> 01:01:13,336
[pilot] <i>1282, we are declaring</i>
<i>an emergency.</i>

1016
01:01:13,419 --> 01:01:15,588
<i>We are descending down to 10,000--</i>

1017
01:01:15,672 --> 01:01:16,839
[continues indistinctly]

1018
01:01:16,923 --> 01:01:18,174
[engine roaring]

1019
01:01:18,800 --> 01:01:21,260
[passenger 1] Almost instantly,
oxygen masks dropped,

1020
01:01:21,344 --> 01:01:23,971
and I realized
there was something really wrong.

1021
01:01:24,055 --> 01:01:25,431
[pilot] <i>We're about to go down.</i>

1022
01:01:26,516 --> 01:01:31,729
[passenger 2] I immediately am disoriented
but quickly turn my head, and I see…

1023
01:01:31,813 --> 01:01:33,564
[whooshing]

1024
01:01:34,857 --> 01:01:36,067
…open sky.

1025
01:01:37,694 --> 01:01:39,862
I was just absolutely in shock.

1026
01:01:42,031 --> 01:01:45,118
[passenger 1] I stuck my 1-year-old's legs
in between my legs,

1027
01:01:45,201 --> 01:01:46,411
and I pinned him down.

1028
01:01:47,495 --> 01:01:49,414
He was screaming the whole time.

1029
01:01:50,039 --> 01:01:51,249
[wind whooshing]

1030
01:01:52,125 --> 01:01:55,169
[passenger 3] When I got the mask on,
there was no airflow.

1031
01:01:57,088 --> 01:01:59,340
Tried to breathe again.
There was no oxygen.

1032
01:01:59,424 --> 01:02:01,175
[tense music playing]

1033
01:02:07,348 --> 01:02:09,559
[passenger 2] And I was looking over
at my daughter,

1034
01:02:09,642 --> 01:02:11,602
seeing her hair being pulled back.

1035
01:02:12,395 --> 01:02:14,981
She was two rows in front of that opening.

1036
01:02:18,192 --> 01:02:21,154
We thought that the next panel
was going to fail

1037
01:02:21,779 --> 01:02:24,532
and that we were not gonna make it out
of that flight alive.

1038
01:02:28,870 --> 01:02:30,580
[passenger 1] The oxygen had depleted.

1039
01:02:30,663 --> 01:02:32,540
We started getting
that dizzy feeling again,

1040
01:02:32,623 --> 01:02:34,333
and my vision started clouding.

1041
01:02:36,043 --> 01:02:39,297
Both my daughters
were looking at me just afraid.

1042
01:02:40,256 --> 01:02:43,384
I hadn't told them it was going to be okay
because I didn't know.

1043
01:02:46,137 --> 01:02:49,932
[passenger 2] I was assuming
that I had minutes with my family.

1044
01:02:50,683 --> 01:02:53,561
So I'm reaching over,
and I put my hand on my wife's back.

1045
01:02:54,896 --> 01:02:57,940
And I just begin to pray.

1046
01:03:00,943 --> 01:03:06,240
[passenger 3] I send a text to my son
to say goodbye.

1047
01:03:06,324 --> 01:03:10,953
Let him know he's been the best thing
that's ever happened to me.

1048
01:03:11,037 --> 01:03:13,039
[tense music continues]

1049
01:03:24,217 --> 01:03:28,137
[passenger 1] I looked out the window,
and the plane dipped under the clouds.

1050
01:03:29,472 --> 01:03:31,098
And we could breathe again.

1051
01:03:34,477 --> 01:03:37,271
I was able to look at my kids and say,
"It's gonna be okay."

1052
01:03:38,189 --> 01:03:39,816
[airplane landing]

1053
01:03:51,577 --> 01:03:53,329
[announcer] <i>We begin with breaking news.</i>

1054
01:03:53,955 --> 01:03:55,998
[newscaster] <i>That breaking news</i>
<i>is out of Oregon.</i>

1055
01:03:56,082 --> 01:03:57,375
<i>And take a look at this.</i>

1056
01:03:57,458 --> 01:04:00,670
{\an8}<i>Video from inside an airliner</i>
<i>still in the air</i>

1057
01:04:00,753 --> 01:04:05,633
{\an8}<i>after a large section of its fuselage</i>
<i>blew out during the flight.</i>

1058
01:04:05,716 --> 01:04:07,593
[pensive music playing]

1059
01:04:07,677 --> 01:04:09,470
{\an8}[Gates] When the Alaska incident occurred,

1060
01:04:09,554 --> 01:04:11,848
{\an8}I was just gathering information
like everyone else.

1061
01:04:11,931 --> 01:04:13,224
{\an8}I didn't know what had happened.

1062
01:04:14,100 --> 01:04:16,978
{\an8}[Ostrower] I was in my kitchen
and my phone blew up.

1063
01:04:17,061 --> 01:04:20,064
{\an8}I knew something was very, very wrong.

1064
01:04:20,147 --> 01:04:23,276
{\an8}[newscaster] <i>National Transportation</i>
<i>Safety Board are investigating this.</i>

1065
01:04:23,359 --> 01:04:26,445
{\an8}At cruise altitude,
we could have lost the aircraft.

1066
01:04:26,529 --> 01:04:30,324
[reporter] <i>The plane is a Boeing 737 MAX</i>
<i>that had just been certified.</i>

1067
01:04:31,158 --> 01:04:33,536
[Gates] What blew off
was what's called a door plug.

1068
01:04:33,619 --> 01:04:34,787
It's not an actual door,

1069
01:04:34,871 --> 01:04:38,791
{\an8}but there is an opening there that
some airlines put an extra door in.

1070
01:04:39,333 --> 01:04:42,003
{\an8}[Ostrower] I called
a close contact at Boeing and told them,

1071
01:04:42,086 --> 01:04:44,297
"This is the kind of stuff
that grounds airplanes."

1072
01:04:44,964 --> 01:04:48,843
[reporter 1] <i>The FAA grounded</i>
<i>all 737 MAX 9s in the U.S.</i>

1073
01:04:48,926 --> 01:04:51,220
[reporter 2] <i>Saying what happened</i>
<i>is likely to exist</i>

1074
01:04:51,304 --> 01:04:53,347
<i>or develop in other planes.</i>

1075
01:04:53,431 --> 01:04:56,017
{\an8}[reporter 3] <i>Boeing denied liability,</i>
<i>saying its products were,</i>

1076
01:04:56,100 --> 01:04:58,519
<i>quote, "improperly maintained, or misused</i>

1077
01:04:58,603 --> 01:05:01,772
<i>by persons and/or entities</i>
<i>other than Boeing."</i>

1078
01:05:01,856 --> 01:05:05,109
{\an8}Our job is to understand
literally everything that has happened.

1079
01:05:05,776 --> 01:05:09,280
[woman] Boeing has not provided us
with the documents

1080
01:05:09,363 --> 01:05:13,284
{\an8}and information that we have requested
numerous times over the past few months.

1081
01:05:13,367 --> 01:05:15,202
{\an8}They talk about transparency,

1082
01:05:15,286 --> 01:05:16,871
{\an8}but certainly, at least so far,

1083
01:05:16,954 --> 01:05:19,999
{\an8}they're not being transparent about
exactly what happened with that plug.

1084
01:05:22,877 --> 01:05:25,504
[Mohawk] I was not surprised
by the door plug incident.

1085
01:05:26,047 --> 01:05:27,924
{\an8}I've been fighting for a couple years,

1086
01:05:28,007 --> 01:05:29,926
{\an8}knowing that something like that
was gonna happen.

1087
01:05:30,009 --> 01:05:31,928
[mellow music playing]

1088
01:05:32,637 --> 01:05:34,889
I was just relieved that nobody died.

1089
01:05:36,891 --> 01:05:40,645
{\an8}It was a nightmare
just having that kind of failure.

1090
01:05:42,855 --> 01:05:48,194
I said to myself, "This is what happens
if you don't hold people accountable."

1091
01:05:50,613 --> 01:05:53,115
{\an8}When the door blowout happened
and I saw that on the news,

1092
01:05:53,199 --> 01:05:57,828
{\an8}I predicted exactly what they were
gonna find during their investigation.

1093
01:05:57,912 --> 01:05:59,997
[music building]

1094
01:06:03,125 --> 01:06:05,962
[Gates] This panel blowing off
the plane incident

1095
01:06:06,045 --> 01:06:09,382
was traced back to a quality lapse
inside the factory.

1096
01:06:11,092 --> 01:06:13,886
[Ostrower] In 2023,
an airplane is coming down the line

1097
01:06:13,970 --> 01:06:17,223
at Boeing's 737 plant near Seattle.

1098
01:06:17,306 --> 01:06:20,685
And it's the airplane
that became Alaska 1282.

1099
01:06:21,477 --> 01:06:23,562
There's chaos elsewhere in the factory

1100
01:06:23,646 --> 01:06:27,525
as Boeing is trying to keep up
with its own increased production

1101
01:06:27,608 --> 01:06:29,527
and dealing with outsourcing.

1102
01:06:31,278 --> 01:06:33,781
[Gates] The investigation discovered

1103
01:06:33,864 --> 01:06:37,952
that the door team at Boeing
had taken off the door plug

1104
01:06:38,035 --> 01:06:42,123
in order to fix things
that suppliers didn't get right.

1105
01:06:43,040 --> 01:06:46,043
To do so, they would have had to remove
four retaining bolts.

1106
01:06:46,627 --> 01:06:47,753
[cranking]

1107
01:06:48,254 --> 01:06:50,840
And there was one photo
in their preliminary report

1108
01:06:50,923 --> 01:06:52,383
that was very telling.

1109
01:06:54,635 --> 01:06:59,765
You could actually see three
of the four corners of the door plug.

1110
01:06:59,849 --> 01:07:05,271
And the retaining bolts that were needed
to secure it in place weren't there.

1111
01:07:05,354 --> 01:07:07,148
[tense music playing]

1112
01:07:07,231 --> 01:07:08,816
They were never put back.

1113
01:07:11,402 --> 01:07:15,239
Afterwards, that plane flew 154 flights.

1114
01:07:15,322 --> 01:07:18,117
And each time it landed or took off,

1115
01:07:18,200 --> 01:07:21,954
the vibrations shifted the door plug
a little tiny bit

1116
01:07:22,038 --> 01:07:27,960
until, eventually,
on January 5th of 2024, it blew out.

1117
01:07:28,044 --> 01:07:29,128
[loud bang]

1118
01:07:29,211 --> 01:07:32,298
{\an8}[man] To date, we have still found
no paperwork documenting

1119
01:07:32,381 --> 01:07:36,177
{\an8}the removal of 8789's left-hand MED plug.

1120
01:07:36,260 --> 01:07:37,303
{\an8}[Lund] That is correct.

1121
01:07:37,386 --> 01:07:40,306
{\an8}Whoever did the work never recorded it.

1122
01:07:40,389 --> 01:07:41,640
Why not?

1123
01:07:41,724 --> 01:07:44,477
Probably because
somebody wanted it done quickly.

1124
01:07:45,686 --> 01:07:48,939
There was a whole system here
that was inadequate.

1125
01:07:51,025 --> 01:07:54,612
{\an8}This was a close call, too close.

1126
01:07:54,695 --> 01:07:57,281
{\an8}[reporter] <i>On Capitol Hill today,</i>
<i>the FAA chief said</i>

1127
01:07:57,364 --> 01:08:00,242
<i>Boeing quality control had clearly failed.</i>

1128
01:08:00,326 --> 01:08:03,287
{\an8}If you don't have that safety culture,
it's hard to make safe airplanes.

1129
01:08:03,370 --> 01:08:05,206
[contemplative music playing]

1130
01:08:05,289 --> 01:08:08,918
[Naoise] Everything that
we had been saying,

1131
01:08:09,001 --> 01:08:10,836
that Boeing's culture was broken

1132
01:08:10,920 --> 01:08:14,507
and that these defective planes
are continuing to fly,

1133
01:08:15,257 --> 01:08:17,343
had just been proved correct.

1134
01:08:19,762 --> 01:08:21,514
That happened two days before

1135
01:08:21,597 --> 01:08:24,767
this deferred prosecution agreement
was about to expire.

1136
01:08:26,227 --> 01:08:27,812
In a way, it's a miracle

1137
01:08:27,895 --> 01:08:30,231
because not only was no one killed,

1138
01:08:30,856 --> 01:08:35,361
now how could they say Boeing have been
good during their probationary period?

1139
01:08:35,444 --> 01:08:36,445
They couldn't.

1140
01:08:37,446 --> 01:08:41,867
[newscaster] <i>The Justice Department</i>
<i>announced Boeing violated a 2021 agreement</i>

1141
01:08:41,951 --> 01:08:44,370
<i>that protected the company</i>
<i>from any charges.</i>

1142
01:08:44,453 --> 01:08:45,788
[reporter] Boeing has responded.

1143
01:08:45,871 --> 01:08:48,290
"We believe that we have honored
the terms of that agreement."

1144
01:08:48,374 --> 01:08:49,500
The question now, though,

1145
01:08:49,583 --> 01:08:52,336
what does a potential punishment
even look like for Boeing?

1146
01:08:52,419 --> 01:08:54,421
Nobody seems to know just yet.

1147
01:08:55,422 --> 01:08:56,757
It opened up everything again.

1148
01:08:56,841 --> 01:08:58,259
[pensive music playing]

1149
01:08:58,342 --> 01:09:02,471
The investigation by the DOJ
and criminal prosecution.

1150
01:09:03,597 --> 01:09:05,307
Everything was back on the table.

1151
01:09:10,604 --> 01:09:14,150
[Boeing lawyer] In the days leading up
to you leaving Boeing,

1152
01:09:14,233 --> 01:09:19,780
did you talk to anyone about the stress
that you were experiencing at work?

1153
01:09:19,864 --> 01:09:21,157
[Barnett] I talked to my doctors.

1154
01:09:21,240 --> 01:09:22,992
[Boeing lawyer] What did your doctor say?

1155
01:09:23,075 --> 01:09:26,453
She said I was gonna have
a heart attack and die,

1156
01:09:26,537 --> 01:09:29,665
or I could have a heart attack and die
in that environment, yes.

1157
01:09:29,748 --> 01:09:31,750
[Boeing lawyer] She said,
during this appointment,

1158
01:09:31,834 --> 01:09:34,211
you could have a heart attack and die
if you don't leave work.

1159
01:09:34,295 --> 01:09:35,212
That's your testimony?

1160
01:09:35,296 --> 01:09:37,673
If I don't get out
of that environment, yes.

1161
01:09:37,756 --> 01:09:39,758
[pensive music playing]

1162
01:09:43,220 --> 01:09:45,222
[birds chirping]

1163
01:09:48,100 --> 01:09:49,560
Can we take a few minutes?

1164
01:09:52,605 --> 01:09:56,901
[Turkewitz] Boeing's counsel took
his deposition for a full seven hours.

1165
01:09:57,776 --> 01:09:59,778
John was really tired.

1166
01:09:59,862 --> 01:10:05,784
He said that he would like to go back home
and resume his deposition at a later date.

1167
01:10:07,328 --> 01:10:10,164
Boeing objected to John's request.

1168
01:10:11,957 --> 01:10:12,917
By this stage,

1169
01:10:13,000 --> 01:10:17,755
John didn't want to do anything that
was going to cause a delay in the trial,

1170
01:10:17,838 --> 01:10:22,635
and that he will just come back
the next morning and we'd finish it up.

1171
01:10:24,303 --> 01:10:26,513
And he went back to his hotel.

1172
01:10:28,724 --> 01:10:30,309
[thunder rumbling]

1173
01:10:30,392 --> 01:10:34,355
The morning of Saturday, March 9th,

1174
01:10:35,356 --> 01:10:37,483
it was storming outside.

1175
01:10:37,566 --> 01:10:40,402
A lot of thunder, lightning,
and really heavy rain.

1176
01:10:41,570 --> 01:10:45,032
By ten o'clock,
we hadn't heard anything from John.

1177
01:10:46,575 --> 01:10:50,162
So when the rain slowed down,
we all drove there.

1178
01:10:50,246 --> 01:10:52,248
[suspenseful music playing]

1179
01:11:01,173 --> 01:11:03,467
[Knowles] Our hopes
as we drove to the hotel

1180
01:11:03,550 --> 01:11:07,179
was that we were gonna just find John
having a medical situation

1181
01:11:07,721 --> 01:11:09,390
but that he was gonna be okay.

1182
01:11:15,479 --> 01:11:18,983
I was thinking he maybe had
a really severe panic attack

1183
01:11:19,066 --> 01:11:20,567
or something like that.

1184
01:11:21,360 --> 01:11:23,445
But when we went inside the hotel,

1185
01:11:24,154 --> 01:11:26,198
the manager took us in the back,

1186
01:11:26,282 --> 01:11:29,535
and the first thing she said to us was,
"I'm sorry for your loss."

1187
01:11:30,119 --> 01:11:35,416
It was really surprising and overwhelming
and just a lot of emotion

1188
01:11:36,083 --> 01:11:39,920
when we're learning
that they had found John in his truck

1189
01:11:40,838 --> 01:11:42,715
with a gunshot wound to the head.

1190
01:11:45,175 --> 01:11:49,346
The police detective believed
it was most likely, uh, suicide.

1191
01:11:51,265 --> 01:11:53,225
[Turkewitz] We had a lot of questions,

1192
01:11:53,976 --> 01:12:00,024
and we wanted to make sure that the police
did a full, complete investigation.

1193
01:12:01,817 --> 01:12:04,737
[Knowles] We wanted to see
his cell phone content.

1194
01:12:05,821 --> 01:12:09,908
Any witnesses that they interviewed,
I'd like to see those witness statements.

1195
01:12:11,327 --> 01:12:13,787
And did they fingerprint
the outside of his truck?

1196
01:12:16,665 --> 01:12:20,127
A former employee
of the U.S. plane maker Boeing,

1197
01:12:20,210 --> 01:12:22,504
who turned whistleblower,
has been found dead.

1198
01:12:22,588 --> 01:12:24,673
[mysterious music playing]

1199
01:12:28,218 --> 01:12:31,096
[Stokes] I talked to him
on the Monday before.

1200
01:12:32,181 --> 01:12:35,142
{\an8}I asked him, I said,
"When are you coming home?"

1201
01:12:36,018 --> 01:12:40,189
He said, "They put my meeting off,
and so I have to stay here."

1202
01:12:40,272 --> 01:12:42,399
"I'll be so glad to get home."

1203
01:12:42,983 --> 01:12:45,652
He said, "I just want to get in my bed."

1204
01:12:48,572 --> 01:12:51,950
And we still have
a lot of unanswered questions.

1205
01:12:53,744 --> 01:12:55,829
We just want to know what happened.

1206
01:13:04,421 --> 01:13:05,672
I was shocked.

1207
01:13:10,803 --> 01:13:15,557
Nobody wants to accept the fact
that John would do this

1208
01:13:15,641 --> 01:13:21,063
so close to completing
what he set out to accomplish,

1209
01:13:21,146 --> 01:13:27,236
bringing awareness to the fact
that Boeing was overlooking quality.

1210
01:13:27,986 --> 01:13:29,822
He was at the finish line.

1211
01:13:30,614 --> 01:13:31,698
Um…

1212
01:13:33,033 --> 01:13:36,245
Being found in his truck like that, um,

1213
01:13:37,579 --> 01:13:41,166
you know, it just…
it just didn't seem to us that

1214
01:13:41,834 --> 01:13:44,628
that was John… the John that we knew.

1215
01:13:44,711 --> 01:13:48,090
[beeping]

1216
01:13:49,258 --> 01:13:51,927
[Knowles] After that,
things got very weird.

1217
01:13:53,011 --> 01:13:55,848
I mean, it was like jumping
into an alternate universe.

1218
01:13:56,515 --> 01:13:59,476
[host] He was obviously under
an enormous amount of scrutiny,

1219
01:13:59,560 --> 01:14:01,687
but was it enough to take his own life?

1220
01:14:02,229 --> 01:14:03,981
That's what everyone wants to know.

1221
01:14:04,606 --> 01:14:07,860
{\an8}We're trying to nail down the timeline
of John Barnett's death

1222
01:14:07,943 --> 01:14:10,195
{\an8}and whether this was indeed a suicide.

1223
01:14:10,279 --> 01:14:12,364
{\an8}People are raising their eyebrows.

1224
01:14:12,948 --> 01:14:15,492
[Knowles] People were saying
that John had been killed

1225
01:14:15,576 --> 01:14:18,370
to silence others from speaking out.

1226
01:14:19,621 --> 01:14:23,876
[woman] <i>He said, "But if anything</i>
<i>happens to me, it's not suicide."</i>

1227
01:14:23,959 --> 01:14:26,920
<i>I know that he did not commit suicide.</i>

1228
01:14:27,004 --> 01:14:28,338
<i>There's no way.</i>

1229
01:14:28,922 --> 01:14:31,049
[Watters] It just doesn't make sense.

1230
01:14:31,133 --> 01:14:32,593
{\an8}Well, that's our feeling too.

1231
01:14:32,676 --> 01:14:37,181
{\an8}And the Charleston City Police are
investigating, and so is the coroner.

1232
01:14:38,474 --> 01:14:41,810
And we're just waiting for
the police department and the coroner

1233
01:14:41,894 --> 01:14:44,855
to release a report
on what they believe happened.

1234
01:14:44,938 --> 01:14:46,482
[tense music playing]

1235
01:14:46,565 --> 01:14:48,817
[Knowles] The atmosphere was just tense.

1236
01:14:49,443 --> 01:14:51,236
And even I started thinking,

1237
01:14:51,320 --> 01:14:54,364
you know, I'm not clear
on what's really happened here.

1238
01:14:56,408 --> 01:14:57,951
[music fades]

1239
01:15:03,499 --> 01:15:07,044
[Meyers] When I had heard about
John Barnett losing his life,

1240
01:15:07,127 --> 01:15:08,420
that was traumatic.

1241
01:15:09,129 --> 01:15:11,131
But what really got me angry

1242
01:15:11,215 --> 01:15:16,720
was finding out how he was treated
by management for years,

1243
01:15:16,803 --> 01:15:19,890
'cause I could identify with that.

1244
01:15:19,973 --> 01:15:21,975
[pensive music playing]

1245
01:15:22,643 --> 01:15:24,228
I was furious.

1246
01:15:26,563 --> 01:15:30,859
They had their chance to fix all this,
and they did nothing.

1247
01:15:33,237 --> 01:15:35,739
So I called <i>The New York Times </i>reporter.

1248
01:15:36,406 --> 01:15:38,033
I just said, use my name

1249
01:15:38,116 --> 01:15:41,495
and let people know
I gave you Boeing documentation.

1250
01:15:48,043 --> 01:15:51,838
Going public was completely
against my grain for the most part.

1251
01:15:51,922 --> 01:15:55,968
I… I'm not, you know,
one that's drawn to a camera.

1252
01:15:56,051 --> 01:16:00,305
Roy Irvin is speaking publicly,
on camera, for the first time.

1253
01:16:01,098 --> 01:16:03,308
John Barnett was a lighthouse.

1254
01:16:03,892 --> 01:16:05,978
When I first started at the company,

1255
01:16:06,061 --> 01:16:08,397
he was pointing people
in the right direction.

1256
01:16:08,480 --> 01:16:10,649
He's continuing to do that now.

1257
01:16:10,732 --> 01:16:15,028
[Emerson] Roy is on a mission
to finish what Barnett started.

1258
01:16:15,112 --> 01:16:18,615
I'm not John,
but somebody needs to keep pushing.

1259
01:16:20,993 --> 01:16:26,248
[Seitz] My family has concerns
about me taking part in this movie,

1260
01:16:27,666 --> 01:16:29,334
but I'm doing it for John.

1261
01:16:33,213 --> 01:16:35,674
[Salehpour exhales sharply]
I was really scared.

1262
01:16:36,800 --> 01:16:42,639
But I promised myself
I will be speaking up,

1263
01:16:42,723 --> 01:16:44,808
regardless of the consequences.

1264
01:16:45,601 --> 01:16:48,687
I'm here today because
I felt that I must come forward.

1265
01:16:48,770 --> 01:16:54,109
{\an8}I have serious concerns about the safety
of the 787 and 777 aircraft.

1266
01:16:54,192 --> 01:16:57,404
{\an8}And I'm willing to take on
professional risk to talk about them.

1267
01:16:58,488 --> 01:17:01,158
[reporter] <i>We've now learned</i>
<i>there is a new whistleblower</i>

1268
01:17:01,241 --> 01:17:02,743
<i>named Sam Mohawk.</i>

1269
01:17:02,826 --> 01:17:04,995
[Mohawk] I put a big target
on my back in there.

1270
01:17:05,078 --> 01:17:06,079
So why'd you do it?

1271
01:17:06,163 --> 01:17:07,623
Because I'm concerned with safety.

1272
01:17:07,706 --> 01:17:09,041
Like, at the end of the day,

1273
01:17:09,124 --> 01:17:10,917
my friends and family
fly on these airplanes.

1274
01:17:12,294 --> 01:17:13,962
[Knowles] Since John's passing,

1275
01:17:14,046 --> 01:17:19,343
we've been contacted by dozens and dozens
and dozens of aviation whistleblowers.

1276
01:17:20,636 --> 01:17:24,806
They all have the same common concerns
that John Barnett had.

1277
01:17:24,890 --> 01:17:28,101
[reporter] The New York Times <i>interviewed</i>
<i>current and former Boeing employees</i>

1278
01:17:28,185 --> 01:17:30,479
<i>who say the workforce has,</i>
<i>quote, "dropped."</i>

1279
01:17:30,562 --> 01:17:33,065
<i>The inspection process has,</i>
<i>quote, "weakened."</i>

1280
01:17:33,148 --> 01:17:37,235
<i>And production speed is being prioritized</i>
<i>over quality control.</i>

1281
01:17:38,195 --> 01:17:40,572
John inspired a lot of people
to come forward.

1282
01:17:42,199 --> 01:17:43,492
[music fades]

1283
01:17:48,330 --> 01:17:50,957
We're headed
to the Charleston County Coroner's Office

1284
01:17:51,041 --> 01:17:52,250
to meet with the coroner

1285
01:17:52,334 --> 01:17:56,380
who reached a conclusion
related to John Barnett's passing.

1286
01:17:56,963 --> 01:18:00,926
They're gonna be disclosing
the results of their investigation,

1287
01:18:01,009 --> 01:18:05,430
including forensic results
and the autopsy results.

1288
01:18:06,014 --> 01:18:07,724
[Knowles] Yeah,
there's a lot of questions.

1289
01:18:09,601 --> 01:18:12,521
{\an8}[Turkewitz] This is gonna be
a very tough day for John's family.

1290
01:18:14,773 --> 01:18:16,358
<i>Can y'all hear me okay?</i>

1291
01:18:16,942 --> 01:18:18,235
Yes, thank you.

1292
01:18:18,819 --> 01:18:21,405
<i>-Good morning, Rodney. Can you hear me?</i>
-Good morning. Yes, ma'am.

1293
01:18:22,114 --> 01:18:28,286
{\an8}So we were notified, um, of a… a death
in a vehicle at the hotel.

1294
01:18:28,370 --> 01:18:31,915
<i>The vehicle, it was locked,</i>
<i>and they had to open it up.</i>

1295
01:18:32,791 --> 01:18:36,503
<i>We then, uh, photographed everything</i>
<i>as we found it.</i>

1296
01:18:37,087 --> 01:18:39,589
[somber music playing]

1297
01:18:40,590 --> 01:18:44,219
<i>The Charleston Police Department came up</i>
<i>with about 80 fingerprints</i>

1298
01:18:44,302 --> 01:18:45,887
<i>that belonged to John.</i>

1299
01:18:48,265 --> 01:18:51,351
There was nothing to let us think
that there had been a struggle

1300
01:18:51,435 --> 01:18:52,811
or anything of that nature.

1301
01:18:55,439 --> 01:18:58,275
We did obtain
the hotel surveillance cameras

1302
01:18:59,025 --> 01:19:02,529
where we can see John
coming into the lobby.

1303
01:19:02,612 --> 01:19:07,325
And then he exits the hotel at 8:37.

1304
01:19:08,243 --> 01:19:13,331
{\an8}His truck is seen backing
into a parking spot at 8:45.

1305
01:19:14,708 --> 01:19:18,962
{\an8}No one is seen entering the vehicle,
exiting the vehicle

1306
01:19:19,045 --> 01:19:21,506
until first responders find him.

1307
01:19:24,217 --> 01:19:27,137
One of the questions
that has been lingering is,

1308
01:19:27,220 --> 01:19:30,891
were you able to determine
that the bullet matched the gun?

1309
01:19:30,974 --> 01:19:36,772
Yes. What was found in the headliner
is what matches to his weapon.

1310
01:19:38,190 --> 01:19:42,277
<i>I think it's consistent</i>
<i>that it's John's weapon,</i>

1311
01:19:42,360 --> 01:19:44,321
<i>he's inside his vehicle,</i>

1312
01:19:44,404 --> 01:19:46,615
<i>and the manner of death is suicide.</i>

1313
01:19:46,698 --> 01:19:49,034
[somber music playing]

1314
01:19:56,917 --> 01:19:58,168
[Stokes] Heart-wrenching…

1315
01:20:00,170 --> 01:20:02,839
to know what, uh, my son had gone through,

1316
01:20:02,923 --> 01:20:05,926
and I saw some of it,

1317
01:20:06,009 --> 01:20:10,972
but I guess I just didn't realize
how much it bothered him.

1318
01:20:12,307 --> 01:20:13,600
[O'Neal] <i>Call me if you need me.</i>

1319
01:20:13,683 --> 01:20:14,643
Thank you.

1320
01:20:14,726 --> 01:20:15,894
[O'Neal] <i>You're welcome.</i>

1321
01:20:15,977 --> 01:20:17,354
-Bye-bye.
-[Rodney] <i>Bye.</i>

1322
01:20:18,814 --> 01:20:19,940
[Stokes sighs]

1323
01:20:20,023 --> 01:20:22,108
Give me a hug after that. [sobs]

1324
01:20:31,243 --> 01:20:32,369
[Knowles] These are the items

1325
01:20:32,452 --> 01:20:36,498
that the Charleston City Police Department
released back to us.

1326
01:20:37,541 --> 01:20:39,835
John's, uh, laptop.

1327
01:20:43,213 --> 01:20:45,090
This was his cell phone.

1328
01:20:47,259 --> 01:20:48,635
And John's notebook.

1329
01:20:49,594 --> 01:20:51,596
[intriguing music playing]

1330
01:20:53,849 --> 01:20:55,517
And written inside there

1331
01:20:55,600 --> 01:21:00,480
is the suicide note that John left
before he took his life.

1332
01:21:04,818 --> 01:21:06,111
"Fuck Boeing."

1333
01:21:07,571 --> 01:21:10,240
"I can't do this any longer. Enough."

1334
01:21:10,866 --> 01:21:14,870
"I pray the motherfuckers
that destroyed my life pay."

1335
01:21:14,953 --> 01:21:17,539
"I pray Boeing pays."

1336
01:21:20,292 --> 01:21:25,255
"Bury me face down so Boeing and
their lying-ass leaders can kiss my ass."

1337
01:21:26,923 --> 01:21:30,427
"To my family and friends,
I found my purpose. I'm at peace."

1338
01:21:30,510 --> 01:21:31,636
"I love you more."

1339
01:21:32,178 --> 01:21:35,849
And then he signed,
John Mitch, aka Swampy.

1340
01:21:38,685 --> 01:21:42,480
[Turkewitz] And it's one of those things
where you just keep asking yourself,

1341
01:21:42,564 --> 01:21:44,691
"What could have I done differently?"

1342
01:21:45,442 --> 01:21:46,318
Yeah.

1343
01:21:48,528 --> 01:21:51,448
But one thing is for sure, and that is,

1344
01:21:52,115 --> 01:21:54,534
you know, Boeing
may not have pulled the trigger,

1345
01:21:54,618 --> 01:21:58,079
but Boeing was responsible
for John's death.

1346
01:22:03,376 --> 01:22:06,421
[reporter] <i>Today, a congressional hearing</i>
<i>is putting the safety and quality</i>

1347
01:22:06,504 --> 01:22:08,715
<i>of Boeing's commercial jets</i>
<i>in the spotlight.</i>

1348
01:22:08,798 --> 01:22:10,425
[pensive music playing]

1349
01:22:10,508 --> 01:22:15,972
After the 737 MAX crashes,
Boeing promised to turn things around.

1350
01:22:17,015 --> 01:22:21,519
{\an8}And whistleblowers
came forward reporting to us

1351
01:22:21,603 --> 01:22:24,773
that, in fact,
there was simply more of the same.

1352
01:22:25,690 --> 01:22:27,692
Improper fastening,

1353
01:22:28,276 --> 01:22:30,820
use of defective and damaged parts,

1354
01:22:31,488 --> 01:22:34,032
lapses in manufacturing quality.

1355
01:22:35,241 --> 01:22:40,372
There is certainly mounting evidence
that a prosecution is justified.

1356
01:22:41,831 --> 01:22:46,503
And we felt more and more
that we had to demand accountability.

1357
01:22:46,586 --> 01:22:52,717
And that's the reason why Dave Calhoun,
the CEO, will be a witness today.

1358
01:22:52,801 --> 01:22:54,803
[pensive music continues]

1359
01:22:58,598 --> 01:23:00,100
[music fades]

1360
01:23:02,352 --> 01:23:04,270
[woman] This is my daughter,
Danielle Moore.

1361
01:23:04,354 --> 01:23:06,564
Danielle was just 24 years old

1362
01:23:07,148 --> 01:23:11,820
when she died on Boeing 737 MAX.

1363
01:23:11,903 --> 01:23:14,114
So, my name is Zipporah Kuria,

1364
01:23:14,197 --> 01:23:16,574
and I lost my dad, Joseph Kuria Waithaka.

1365
01:23:17,575 --> 01:23:20,996
As the agencies involved
in making decisions that matter,

1366
01:23:21,079 --> 01:23:22,163
if you fail to prosecute,

1367
01:23:22,247 --> 01:23:26,876
if you fail to teach history a real lesson
of justice, and it repeats itself,

1368
01:23:26,960 --> 01:23:28,586
how will you live with yourselves?

1369
01:23:28,670 --> 01:23:30,672
-[indistinct chatter]
-[cameras clicking]

1370
01:23:30,755 --> 01:23:32,257
[somber music playing]

1371
01:23:43,184 --> 01:23:45,854
[parent of deceased] You destroyed
our son's life,

1372
01:23:45,937 --> 01:23:47,939
and you killed our children.

1373
01:23:48,773 --> 01:23:52,610
We will never have
a chance to see and hug them

1374
01:23:52,694 --> 01:23:55,238
just the way you do with your family.

1375
01:23:55,321 --> 01:23:57,198
Give us the answer.

1376
01:23:57,282 --> 01:23:59,409
[woman] Stop putting profit over people.

1377
01:23:59,492 --> 01:24:01,202
[parent of deceased]
Think of the six minutes

1378
01:24:01,286 --> 01:24:03,705
of my daughter's last breath on the plane.

1379
01:24:04,706 --> 01:24:07,584
Six minutes that she called for me.

1380
01:24:07,667 --> 01:24:11,087
{\an8}Five years ago, 346 innocent people

1381
01:24:11,171 --> 01:24:14,049
{\an8}lost their lives
in two preventable tragedies.

1382
01:24:14,758 --> 01:24:19,220
In the wake of those tragedies,
Boeing promised to turn itself around.

1383
01:24:19,804 --> 01:24:24,059
And for a while, some sort of believed
that Boeing might have changed.

1384
01:24:24,142 --> 01:24:26,186
But then this past January,

1385
01:24:26,269 --> 01:24:31,066
the facade
literally blew off the hollow shell

1386
01:24:31,149 --> 01:24:34,194
that had been Boeing's promises
to the world.

1387
01:24:35,236 --> 01:24:39,699
The issues before us today
are a matter of life and death.

1388
01:24:39,783 --> 01:24:43,787
{\an8}And this hearing is a moment of reckoning.

1389
01:24:45,330 --> 01:24:50,043
I would like to welcome in particular
some of the members of the audience

1390
01:24:50,126 --> 01:24:51,669
who are here today.

1391
01:24:52,378 --> 01:24:54,297
{\an8}The family of John Barnett,

1392
01:24:55,215 --> 01:25:00,136
{\an8}his mother, Vicky Stokes,
and brother, Rodney Barnett,

1393
01:25:01,012 --> 01:25:04,516
a Boeing whistleblower
who committed suicide

1394
01:25:04,599 --> 01:25:08,394
under the immense pressure
that the company put on him

1395
01:25:08,478 --> 01:25:10,271
for raising safety claims.

1396
01:25:12,232 --> 01:25:17,153
David Calhoun has served as President
and Chief Executive Officer of Boeing

1397
01:25:17,237 --> 01:25:19,280
since January 2020,

1398
01:25:19,364 --> 01:25:23,576
{\an8}and he began his career
at General Electric Company, GE,

1399
01:25:23,660 --> 01:25:26,454
{\an8}where he spent 26 years.

1400
01:25:27,872 --> 01:25:31,042
{\an8}Mr. Calhoun, it seems like every other day

1401
01:25:31,126 --> 01:25:35,004
{\an8}news breaks of a new problem
or safety concern with Boeing aircraft.

1402
01:25:35,088 --> 01:25:38,550
So what are the root causes
of this systemic failure?

1403
01:25:38,633 --> 01:25:41,928
{\an8}How are you gonna fix it?
And who should we hold accountable?

1404
01:25:42,011 --> 01:25:44,722
{\an8}Does the buck stop with you
or with somebody else?

1405
01:25:45,431 --> 01:25:51,604
The contributing causes to most everything
outside of the Alaska accident

1406
01:25:52,147 --> 01:25:56,442
relate to a variety of inputs
that are downstream

1407
01:25:56,526 --> 01:26:00,655
with respect to operating and maintenance
and et cetera with an airplane.

1408
01:26:00,738 --> 01:26:01,739
Um…

1409
01:26:02,365 --> 01:26:04,951
Alaska was a manufacturing defect.

1410
01:26:05,034 --> 01:26:08,538
I'm not aware of any others that were. Um…

1411
01:26:08,621 --> 01:26:11,583
[Blumenthal] The suppliers
of parts and components,

1412
01:26:12,792 --> 01:26:14,377
Boeing blames them.

1413
01:26:15,253 --> 01:26:20,550
The workers whose jobs are at stake,
Boeing blames them.

1414
01:26:21,217 --> 01:26:26,681
The pilots who fly these planes,
Boeing blames them.

1415
01:26:26,764 --> 01:26:30,018
Boeing blames everyone except itself.

1416
01:26:30,685 --> 01:26:33,771
This… So much of this relates
to an untrained workforce.

1417
01:26:33,855 --> 01:26:37,108
I… I… It's all about that, honestly.

1418
01:26:38,693 --> 01:26:43,323
Uh, Mr. Calhoun, what is it that…
that you get paid currently?

1419
01:26:44,324 --> 01:26:47,869
Senator, that's well disclosed
in our proxy documents,

1420
01:26:47,952 --> 01:26:50,288
uh, in… in each of the years
that I've been employed.

1421
01:26:50,371 --> 01:26:51,998
{\an8}Yeah, but what is it?

1422
01:26:52,081 --> 01:26:54,959
-It's a big number, sir.
-[Hawley] Well, let me help you out.

1423
01:26:55,043 --> 01:26:57,128
{\an8}It's $32.8 million this year.
Does that sound right?

1424
01:26:57,212 --> 01:26:58,463
Yes, it does.

1425
01:26:58,546 --> 01:27:02,342
That's a 45% increase over last year.
Does that sound right?

1426
01:27:02,425 --> 01:27:03,968
[smacks lips] Yes, it does.

1427
01:27:04,052 --> 01:27:06,387
[Hawley] What is it
you get paid to do exactly?

1428
01:27:07,013 --> 01:27:08,681
I get paid to run the Boeing Company.

1429
01:27:08,765 --> 01:27:12,310
{\an8}Yeah. Is quality part
of your compensation package?

1430
01:27:12,393 --> 01:27:16,231
Because we've had multiple whistleblowers
come before this committee and allege

1431
01:27:16,314 --> 01:27:20,526
{\an8}that Boeing is cutting every possible
corner on quality and safety,

1432
01:27:20,610 --> 01:27:22,862
{\an8}not just in the past but now.

1433
01:27:22,946 --> 01:27:25,365
They've alleged that you've eliminated
safety inspections,

1434
01:27:25,448 --> 01:27:28,326
that there are fewer inspectors
doing quality inspections out there.

1435
01:27:28,409 --> 01:27:31,829
They've alleged that
when they raised issues and concerns,

1436
01:27:31,913 --> 01:27:34,457
they were reassigned,
they were retaliated against,

1437
01:27:34,540 --> 01:27:36,209
they were physically threatened.

1438
01:27:36,793 --> 01:27:39,504
{\an8}That doesn't sound like
attention to quality to me.

1439
01:27:39,587 --> 01:27:44,300
{\an8}And yet you're getting paid
$33 million a year.

1440
01:27:44,384 --> 01:27:46,552
[pensive music playing]

1441
01:27:50,640 --> 01:27:53,601
{\an8}[Gelles] Boeing is
in worse shape than ever,

1442
01:27:53,685 --> 01:27:56,479
and what's really hard to accept

1443
01:27:57,063 --> 01:28:00,566
is that the executives
who made all those decisions

1444
01:28:00,650 --> 01:28:02,694
are worth hundreds of millions of dollars.

1445
01:28:04,028 --> 01:28:05,780
Boeing is in shambles,

1446
01:28:06,364 --> 01:28:10,493
and the very small group of men
who got the company there

1447
01:28:10,576 --> 01:28:13,913
are some of the richest people
in the world.

1448
01:28:15,832 --> 01:28:19,168
[Gates] There have been many executives
who have come into Boeing,

1449
01:28:19,711 --> 01:28:21,754
come into companies across America,

1450
01:28:22,630 --> 01:28:26,384
who see their jobs as cost-cutting,

1451
01:28:27,969 --> 01:28:31,597
squeezing money out of the company
to increase profits.

1452
01:28:32,348 --> 01:28:35,226
{\an8}You're strip-mining it.
You're strip-mining Boeing.

1453
01:28:35,310 --> 01:28:37,395
{\an8}It was one of the greatest
American companies ever.

1454
01:28:37,478 --> 01:28:40,106
{\an8}It has employed
thousands of people in my state,

1455
01:28:40,189 --> 01:28:44,319
{\an8}and you are strip-mining it for profit,
shareholder value,

1456
01:28:44,402 --> 01:28:45,903
{\an8}and you're being rewarded for it.

1457
01:28:45,987 --> 01:28:48,448
{\an8}You got a huge raise, a huge increase.

1458
01:28:48,990 --> 01:28:53,286
{\an8}So, it's working out great for you,
not for the American people.

1459
01:28:54,329 --> 01:28:57,373
Many of these executives
have no connection

1460
01:28:57,457 --> 01:28:58,958
to the product they're making.

1461
01:28:59,042 --> 01:29:01,669
They were hired because
they had some kind of business degree.

1462
01:29:01,753 --> 01:29:03,880
It's a characteristic
that successful companies have

1463
01:29:03,963 --> 01:29:05,882
and unsuccessful companies don't have.

1464
01:29:05,965 --> 01:29:07,800
{\an8}This year, we're at 47 a month.

1465
01:29:07,884 --> 01:29:10,053
{\an8}We're gonna be taking it up to 52 a month.

1466
01:29:10,136 --> 01:29:12,597
{\an8}That forecast is getting
more and more robust every day,

1467
01:29:12,680 --> 01:29:14,432
{\an8}and if we can get some help
from the Congress,

1468
01:29:14,515 --> 01:29:17,018
we will ultimately realize those gains.

1469
01:29:17,810 --> 01:29:19,604
[air traffic controller]
<i>Frontier Flight 2688,</i>

1470
01:29:19,687 --> 01:29:22,565
<i>Portland Tower, runway 2-8 Left,</i>
<i>cleared for takeoff.</i>

1471
01:29:22,648 --> 01:29:24,692
[pilot] <i>2-8 Left, cleared for takeoff.</i>

1472
01:29:25,860 --> 01:29:28,488
[Meyers] Boeing likes
to attack the symptoms.

1473
01:29:29,155 --> 01:29:33,159
Some part flies off an airplane,
or an engine catches fire,

1474
01:29:33,951 --> 01:29:39,415
they want to go after a quick fix,
and then they can say they did something.

1475
01:29:39,499 --> 01:29:44,754
Boeing never attacks the disease,
which is their leadership model.

1476
01:29:46,881 --> 01:29:49,509
Pursuit of money at all costs

1477
01:29:50,134 --> 01:29:54,180
rather than making
the greatest airplane in the world.

1478
01:29:56,766 --> 01:29:59,811
[Gelles] In most companies,
the stakes aren't people's lives.

1479
01:30:00,395 --> 01:30:02,563
But at Boeing, they are.

1480
01:30:05,483 --> 01:30:09,529
When the quality of Boeing suffers,
people die.

1481
01:30:12,198 --> 01:30:14,742
[Gates] We all need safe air travel.

1482
01:30:15,743 --> 01:30:17,328
We need safe airplanes.

1483
01:30:18,371 --> 01:30:20,331
Boeing needs to be held accountable.

1484
01:30:22,917 --> 01:30:24,961
[pensive music playing]

1485
01:30:59,412 --> 01:31:02,707
[Naoise] They really just wanted
to get rid of us.

1486
01:31:02,790 --> 01:31:07,044
But I don't think I can walk away
and just say, "There's nothing we can do,"

1487
01:31:07,128 --> 01:31:08,337
and put my hands in the air.

1488
01:31:09,964 --> 01:31:12,842
There's been no justice,
no accountability.

1489
01:31:14,010 --> 01:31:15,511
It makes me so angry.

1490
01:31:17,430 --> 01:31:20,183
And I think that we have to keep fighting.

1491
01:31:22,310 --> 01:31:25,313
{\an8}It can't be allowed to just stay this way.

1492
01:31:27,523 --> 01:31:30,067
Something has to change.

1493
01:31:32,945 --> 01:31:34,947
[solemn music playing]

1494
01:33:07,456 --> 01:33:09,417
[music fades]